### Future ideas {.unlisted}

Idea: estimating the effective analytic search space to contextualize p-values
One limitation of counting reported p-values is that the raw number of p-values does not fully capture the degree of analytic flexibility available to investigators. Many studies evaluate families of related hypotheses (e.g., multiple outcomes, multiple predictors, subgroup analyses, or alternative model specifications), creating an implicit search space in which statistically significant findings may emerge even in the absence of a true signal. A complementary metric would estimate the effective number of independent tests implied by the study design, rather than relying solely on the observed count of reported p-values.

This could be operationalized by extracting structural features of each article, including the number of distinct outcomes, exposures, model families, subgroup analyses, and sensitivity analyses. These components could be combined into a heuristic estimate of the effective analytic search space, incorporating shrinkage factors to account for correlation among related tests (e.g., multiple phenotypes or repeated models applied to the same dataset). The resulting metric would not aim to perfectly reconstruct the investigator’s analytic decision tree, but rather to approximate the magnitude of inferential flexibility available within the study.

At scale, this framework could allow articles to be categorized into broad bands (e.g., low, moderate, high analytic flexibility) and compared against observed reporting patterns such as multiplicity correction, prespecification of primary endpoints, or use of hierarchical testing strategies. This approach may provide a more nuanced understanding of the evidentiary strength of statistically significant findings, particularly in exploratory observational studies where multiple related hypotheses are evaluated without formal adjustment for multiplicity.


