### Prompt Engineering Strategy for JAAD Statistical Extraction {.unlisted}

#### Overview {.unlisted}

The LLM prompts in this pipeline are designed to function as a
structured data extraction protocol for statistical reporting
characteristics in JAAD articles and supplements.

Rather than simply asking the model to list p-values, the prompts guide
the model to produce traceable, reproducible, and auditable outputs that
align with the goals of meta-research on analytic flexibility,
multiplicity, and statistical reporting practices.

The approach treats the LLM as a semi-structured abstraction layer
between PDF documents and downstream analytic datasets.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Key Design Principles

1. Structured Output Schema

Prompts require the model to return machine-readable JSON with a
predefined schema:

{ “p_value_catalog”: [ { “p_value”: ““,”description”: ““,”location”:
““,”source_type”: “” } ], “p_values”: [], “n_p_values”: 0 }

This ensures:

-   consistent formatting across articles
-   deterministic downstream parsing
-   compatibility with existing QC pipelines
-   transparency of extracted statistical evidence

------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Context-Aware p-value Extraction

The prompts instruct the model to extract only explicitly reported
p-values from:

-   main text
-   tables
-   figure legends
-   figure annotations
-   supplemental materials

The model is instructed not to infer p-values from:

-   confidence intervals
-   asterisk notation
-   narrative claims of significance
-   bolded values
-   implied hypothesis tests

------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Explicit Exclusion of Statistical Threshold Statements

A key challenge in automated extraction is distinguishing between:

inferential result: P = .047
reporting convention: significance P < .05

Prompts explicitly instruct the model to exclude threshold language such
as:

-   statistical significance was set at P < .05
-   alpha = 0.05
-   considered statistically significant at P < .05

This reduces false positives from Methods sections.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Catalog-Based Representation of p-values

Instead of returning only a flat list of p-values, the model is asked to
produce a catalog linking each p-value to its empirical context.

Example:

{ “p_value”: “.13”, “description”: “age”, “location”: “Table 1”,
“source_type”: “table” }

This mirrors manual QC conventions such as:

“.13” # age – Table 1

Benefits:

-   improves interpretability
-   reduces hallucinated values
-   enables easier reconciliation with manual QC
-   distinguishes duplicate p-values
-   facilitates error detection

------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Alignment with Meta-Research Objectives

The prompts also instruct the model to identify analytic structure
characteristics relevant to statistical reporting practices:

“analytic_structure”: { “repeated_model_family”: false,
“baseline_tables”: false, “sensitivity_analyses”: false,
“subgroup_analyses”: false, “multiple_outcomes”: false,
“high_dimensional_screen”: false }

These indicators support evaluation of:

-   analytic flexibility
-   multiplicity
-   model specification patterns
-   sensitivity analysis practices
-   dimensionality of hypothesis testing space

------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Parallel Structure for Main Articles and Supplements

Separate prompts are used for:

-   main article PDFs
-   supplemental PDFs

Both prompts use the same JSON schema, allowing outputs to be merged
seamlessly during downstream processing.

Supplements frequently contain:

-   additional model families
-   sensitivity analyses
-   subgroup analyses
-   expanded baseline tables
-   additional hypothesis tests

------------------------------------------------------------------------

7. Reproducibility and Auditability

Requiring short descriptions for each p-value creates an interpretable
link between extracted values and their scientific context.

This improves:

-   QC traceability
-   error detection
-   reviewer interpretability
-   reproducibility of meta-research findings

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Conceptual Role of the LLM in the Pipeline

The prompts operationalize the LLM as a structured extractor analogous
to:

-   systematic review data abstraction forms
-   CONSORT checklist extraction
-   PRISMA evidence tables
-   risk-of-bias instruments

The resulting dataset supports evaluation of statistical reporting
practices across JAAD articles and supplements.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Summary

The prompt design emphasizes:

-   deterministic structured output
-   context-aware statistical extraction
-   explicit handling of threshold vs inferential p-values
-   compatibility with manual QC workflows
-   scalability across large document collections

This approach improves both precision and interpretability relative to
naive regex-based p-value extraction while maintaining reproducibility
required for meta-research.


