### Study inclusion criteria {.unlisted}

Articles were included in the analytic corpus if they demonstrated evidence of inferential statistical analysis, defined as the presence of ≥1 reported p-value, ≥1 confidence interval, or an identifiable Methods section consistent with hypothesis-testing research. Articles labeled as research by journal taxonomy but lacking inferential statistical structure (e.g., narrative reviews, consensus statements, descriptive summaries without formal testing) were excluded, as they do not contribute to the analytic search space relevant to multiplicity and statistical inference.


### Project Context Summary (JAAD Statistical Reporting Study) {.unlisted}
We are developing a hybrid pipeline to characterize the analytic search space in dermatology research articles (JAAD) by systematically extracting and classifying statistical reporting features from main articles and supplemental materials. The overall goal is to move beyond simply counting p-values and instead describe how studies structure inference, including multiplicity handling, threshold-based decision rules, preregistration practices, and analytic flexibility. The project combines three complementary layers:
Deterministic extraction (HTML parsing) – primary method for identifying explicitly printed p-values in article text and tables using reproducible regex-based logic.
Manual QC dataset – curated validation set capturing p-values and methodological features with structured annotation fields.
LLM-based PDF parsing layer – applied primarily to PDFs and supplements where formatting, tables, figures, or symbolic notation limit deterministic extraction.
We have been refining the LLM prompts to ensure alignment with the manual QC schema and deterministic outputs. The prompts now extract structured information about explicitly reported p-values, normalize numeric values, and capture key statistical design features such as confidence interval reporting, multiplicity correction, Bayesian methods, preregistration statements, and analytic structure indicators (e.g., repeated model families, sensitivity analyses, subgroup analyses, high-dimensional screening patterns).
A key conceptual improvement has been separating several commonly conflated constructs:
mentions_multiple_testing → explicit correction for multiplicity (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR)
reports_nominal_p_values → explicit statement that p-values are unadjusted or descriptive
mentions_significance_threshold → whether authors define a decision threshold (e.g., P < .05)
significance_threshold_value → numeric alpha threshold if explicitly stated
is_preregistered → prospective protocol or trial registration (explicitly excluding registry data sources such as SEER or institutional registries)
We also preserved supplement-specific capabilities such as extracting symbol-defined significance markers (e.g., *, †) when explicitly linked to thresholds, since supplements frequently encode statistical results using footnotes rather than explicit numeric p-values.
At this point we have:
• fully harmonized main article and supplement prompts
• consistent JSON schemas aligned with the downstream parser
• expanded statistical reporting variables to better characterize analytic flexibility
• clarified definitions to reduce drift and over-calling (particularly preregistration and multiplicity control)
Next steps will likely include:
• validating output consistency across several test articles
• confirming compatibility with parse_llm_json()
• evaluating whether analytic structure variables behave as expected
• integrating outputs into the article-level dataset for comparison with deterministic extraction
• summarizing reporting patterns across JAAD issues
The overarching objective is to produce a reproducible framework for describing how statistical inference is operationalized in dermatology research, with particular attention to multiplicity, thresholding behavior, and analytic search space structure.

