### LLM-Based P-Value Extraction Pipeline v3 {.unlisted}

#### Overview {.unlisted}

Over the past several days we substantially redesigned the LLM extraction framework for the Open Inference Project. The original article-level extraction pipeline evolved into a modular, experimentally versioned workflow that separates extraction, scoring, prompt optimization, and semantic reconciliation.

The redesign was motivated by three objectives:

1. maximize recall of statistical results;
2. permit controlled evaluation of alternative prompts and input representations;
3. separate occurrence extraction from semantic consolidation so that each stage can be optimized independently.

#### Major architectural changes {.unlisted}

##### 1. Versioned prompt architecture {.unlisted}

Moved prompts out of the production pipeline into version-controlled prompt directories.

```
scripts/
    LLM Prompts/
        gpt55_pvalue_v01/
        gpt55_pvalue_v02/
        gpt55_pvalue_v03/
```

Each prompt version now contains independent task prompts including:

- main article p-value extraction
- supplement extraction
- analytic metadata extraction
- semantic reconciliation

This allows prompt evolution without modifying pipeline code.

---

##### 2. Prompt optimization pipeline {.unlisted}

Created a dedicated prompt optimization workflow consisting of

- run_llm_prompt_optimization.R
- Prompt Optimization Scoring.qmd
- score_prompt_optimization_runs.R

Rather than repeatedly processing the complete JAAD corpus, prompt development is performed on curated calibration article sets.

Current calibration sets include

- fast optimization (5 articles)
- full optimization (6 articles including the monster article)
- monster-only testing

Raw outputs are preserved so that new scoring logic can be applied without rerunning inference.

---

##### 3. Structured HTML input pipeline {.unlisted}

Added an alternative article representation generated from structured HTML.

Three input representations can now be evaluated independently:

- original PDF
- structured HTML-derived Markdown
- structured HTML plus recovered figure images

The HTML representation preserves

- section hierarchy
- tables
- captions
- table footnotes
- figure captions

allowing direct comparison between document representations while holding prompts constant.

---

##### 4. Input bake-off framework {.unlisted}

Created a controlled benchmarking pipeline comparing multiple document representations using identical prompts and models.

Each article is evaluated under

- PDF
- structured HTML
- structured HTML plus figures

while keeping supplement inputs constant.

This permits direct measurement of the contribution of article representation to extraction performance.

---

##### 5. Separation of extraction and scoring {.unlisted}

Extraction is now completely separated from evaluation.

Inference produces versioned raw RDS files.

Independent scoring scripts compute

- recall
- precision
- duplicate surplus
- novel false positives
- article-level summaries

without requiring another API call.

---

##### 6. Semantic reconciliation {.unlisted}

Introduced a second-stage semantic reconciliation pipeline.

Rather than asking the model to identify unique statistical results directly, the extraction model first identifies every statistical occurrence.

A second inference stage then groups semantically identical statistical occurrences into canonical statistical results.

Two reconciliation strategies are currently being evaluated:

A. JSON-only reconciliation

- occurrence JSON only

B. Full-context reconciliation

- occurrence JSON
- original article context
- supplements
- recovered figure images

Both strategies use identical prompts and models to isolate the value of additional article context.

---

##### 7. Long-context instrumentation {.unlisted}

Added detailed accounting of reconciliation requests including

- number of extracted occurrences
- number of exact candidates
- number of attached context files
- estimated input tokens
- estimated tokens per candidate

This provides empirical measurements of long-context scaling and informs future batching strategies.

---

#### Current workflow {.unlisted}

Current architecture is now

Article
↓

LLM occurrence extraction

↓

Raw occurrence objects

↓

Scoring

↓

Semantic reconciliation

↓

Canonical statistical results

↓

Final benchmarking

This separates high-recall extraction from semantic interpretation while allowing each stage to evolve independently.

#### Current status {.unlisted}

The pipeline is now capable of

- version-controlled prompt optimization
- controlled document-input benchmarking
- independent scoring
- semantic reconciliation
- long-context performance evaluation

The remaining work focuses primarily on evaluating reconciliation performance and determining whether article context materially improves semantic consolidation over JSON-only reconciliation.


