- Uses the longest observed form as the display name

This is a practical heuristic and may merge homonyms or fail on unusual naming structures. It should therefore be considered an exploratory search aid rather than a validated author-identity system.

### Important implementation notes {.unlisted}

Several issues were identified and corrected during development:

#### Shiny validation namespace {.unlisted}

Because another loaded package can mask `validate()`, all Shiny validation calls should use:

```r
shiny::validate(
  shiny::need(...)
)
```

#### Case-insensitive regular expressions {.unlisted}

`stringr::str_remove()` does not take `ignore_case` directly. Use:

```r
stringr::regex(
  "...",
  ignore_case = TRUE
)
```

#### Shiny action-button icons {.unlisted}

Use:

```r
shiny::icon()
```

rather than `bsicons::bs_icon()` inside `shiny::actionButton()`.

#### Navbar arguments {.unlisted}

Do not pass a custom CSS class as a named argument directly to `bslib::page_navbar()`, because it can be interpreted as a tab. The app styling instead targets the navbar and tab structure through CSS.

#### Selectize refresh {.unlisted}

The matching-article Selectize input should be cleared before new choices are loaded so that stale article selections do not persist after changing title or author filters.

#### Table contextualization order {.unlisted}

The `table_articles` object must be created only after `analytic_articles` has received its contextual percentile fields. Otherwise the Article Table will fail because fields such as:

```text
p_burden_percentile_overall
observed_search_space_0_100
```

do not yet exist.

#### Table height {.unlisted}

The Article Table required explicit height control at all three layers:

1. The `bslib` card
2. The card body
3. The `DT` htmlwidget

The current implementation uses a viewport-relative card height and a viewport-relative internal table scroll region so that the table fills most of the visible screen.

