### Reproducibility, caveats, and evaluation standards {.unlisted}

- Distinguish **model occurrence count**, **exact candidate count**, **canonical unique-result count**, and **historically verified main/supplement p-value entries**. They have different denominators and are not interchangeable.
- Preserve each original p-value string and operator. Operators specify inequalities/bounds, so e.g. `<.001` must not be represented as an exact `0.001` result. Keep original printed precision when possible.
- `P < .05` as a *significance convention* is a threshold, not an observed study-result p-value. Figure asterisks count as results only when symbols are explicitly and unambiguously linked to an outcome/comparison; do not propagate a generic legend to every panel.
- Reconcile identical scientific results even when repeated in different places or formatted differently; do not merge distinct outcomes/models/timepoints because their numerical p-values coincide.
- Preserve candidate ID accounting, reasons for excluded candidates, run-specific sources, hashes and prompt/model versions; avoid overwriting runs or historical checkpoints.
- Clearly identify **AI-assisted human adjudication** as the manual gold-standard process; the draft and automatic classifications remain proposals until actually reviewed.
- A new ChatGPT interaction, different model or PDF-vs-HTML input path is not automatically an apples-to-apples reproduction of GPT-5.6 v3 API performance. Any prompt comparison must document version, model, inference environment and input arm; use development articles to identify error patterns and fresh held-out articles for a future honest estimate of generalization.
- Default to the simplest interface that preserves scientific evidence and reproducibility: annotated `.R` records plus automatic files and a brief explicit provenance header, rather than an elaborate click-by-click QC UI.

