- Completeness states are separate: source_fidelity, semantic_reconciliation, schema_validation, and human_verification. Never treat a model draft as human verified.

STAGE 01 — SOURCE INVENTORY AND PROVENANCE PREFLIGHT
Record canonical article ID (S-prefixed PII if present), title, first author, byline and bibliographic metadata FROM ARTICLE, all uploaded filenames, sizes, SHA256, role, format and available source page/table structures. Inspect article and supplement manifests for missing numbered supplements. Snapshot THIS EXACT START_HERE byte-for-byte in run_provenance/prompt_snapshots; if available, snapshot separately supplied actual prompt files but DO NOT invent inaccessible internal instructions. Record model identifier, observable thinking effort only if actually available, UTC timestamp, runtime, source hashes. Create per-stage status and record blockers. Treat duplicate format renderings as one scientific source.

STAGE 02 — TWO INDEPENDENT MAIN-SOURCE EXTRACTIONS
A. HTML, if present: parse semantic HTML tables DOM/tr/td/th with rowspan/colspan awareness; collect numbered tables, row label hierarchy, subgroup, p-value cells, operators, statistical model-column headings, footnotes. Preserve exact row ordering and HTML locator; avoid unrelated website navigation/analytics script text. Capture narrative independently as HTML text if feasible.
B. PDF: INDEPENDENTLY parse article tables by positions AND inspect page images wherever ambiguous. Collect source-specific table rows, p cells, row and column headings, page coordinates and labels WITHOUT borrowing HTML labels or counts. Independently scan narrative, abstract, captions and graphical symbols; figures can be inspected from PDFs and separately supplied JPG/PNG without counting identical depictions twice.
C. Both streams must be saved separately even when they agree. Never use expected counts from the other stream to fabricate PDF detections.

STAGE 03 — SOURCE-FIDELITY COMPARISON AND ADJUDICATION
Match HTML/PDF MAIN table records by table ID + normalized row hierarchy + column context + p operator/value. Report matched rows, HTML-only, PDF-only, value disagreements, label disagreements, and uncertain matches. Compare both counts PER table and row-level values. Save source_reconciliation/main_html_pdf_comparison.json plus discrepancy ledger listing raw evidence and source locators. Resolve by examining original PDF page/figure and, where appropriate, HTML table; confidence and reason must be explicit. If inaccessible, leave unresolved; do not promote source completeness. Once resolved, create one canonical main-table occurrence per PRINTED CELL (not per format); separately account for abstract/narrative/figure reports. If only one main format exists, state single-source confidence and do not pretend a dual-format comparison was performed.

STAGE 04 — SUPPLEMENT INVENTORY AND EXTRACTION
For each numbered supplement/table separately, prefer source-native DOCX structured tables when provided; independently compare with PDF if both representations exist, deduplicating logical cells. For PDF-only supplements use page-image/table-aware reading with explicit column and section context. Extract each p-value plus row name, parent covariate (for indented factor levels), outcome, risk set/cohort, adjustment, model family, reference group, estimate/CI if printed, page/table/cell. Preserve operator and source order. Note empty/no-p-value tables explicitly. For very long Cox tables process in bounded blocks per outcome/model with completeness counts and carry state between blocks. Don't conflate different coefficient tests with an omnibus category p-value. BEFORE extracting p cells, enumerate all explicit subsection headers, page-break continuations, populations, model types, table titles and transition row boundaries into a SOURCE BLOCK REGISTRY; review PDF page images whenever an endpoint heading sits near a page break. Assign block IDs and paragraph/row spans, freeze the registry as source_reconciliation/source_blocks.json, and extract against that registry rather than carrying forward the most recently seen endpoint. On transition, reset endpoint/population/model and parent-predictor context; never silently inherit the previous block into a new numbered table. For descriptive cohort tables, endpoint is `descriptive_characteristics` (not the survival endpoint of the previous table). If a PDF heading is missing or ambiguous, set outcome to unknown and flag unanchored/partial; do not guess based on neighboring coefficients.


STAGE 04B — MANDATORY BLOCK-HEADER CHECKPOINT (BEFORE ANY LABELED OCCURRENCE)
The source_block_registry is scientific evidence, not a post-hoc field generated from already-assigned P labels. For each numbered table, partition every numbered section according to literal heading and page/row boundaries. Include table ID; block ID; printed heading; normalized endpoint (or descriptive_characteristics); cohort/analysis unit; stage of disease; treatment restriction; adjustment; model family; source locator; start/end positions; heading confidence; and how continuations were recognized. Each p cell must belong to exactly ONE block. Every block must have a documented source heading or a documented continuation link to an earlier heading. Force a semantic-reset when transitioning table IDs even if the next table has no statistical endpoint. Audit the first, last and page-break-adjacent rows of EVERY block; flags become unresolved rather than inheriting the preceding endpoint.

STAGE 05B — INDEPENDENT LABEL AUDIT (MANDATORY BEFORE RECONCILIATION)
Build `reports/label_anchor_validation.json` from comparison of the frozen source_block_registry and labeled_occurrences, not from the same labeling logic. Test: coverage 100%; distinct blocks do not overlap; all entries' endpoint/population/adjustment/model labels agree with the literal source heading; parent covariates reset on new parent group; descriptive tables cannot masquerade as survival outcomes. On any failure, repair from the article image/text and re-freeze both stages; otherwise mark PARTIAL and add the specific P IDs to the unresolved ledger. No downstream count is considered valid while mis-labeled entries persist.

STAGE 05 — LABELED OCCURRENCE REGISTRY (MANDATORY BEFORE RECONCILIATION)
Build exactly one stable P000001... entry per canonical logical source occurrence in labeled_occurrences.json with fields: occurrence_id, source_component, source_order, reported_p, result_label, outcome, predictor_or_contrast, population, analysis_method, model_specification, adjustment_context, table_section, source_location, evidence_text, label_status (supported/partial/unresolved), plus optional source_variant_matches, regression_covariate_family, reference_level, effect_estimate, confidence_interval. Every R-vector p must refer to a registry P ID; preserve ambiguity explicitly. Each P entry MUST additionally carry source_block_id, heading_anchor_text, endpoint_anchor_status, and label_origin (heading/table row/narrative/inferred/unknown), with source-literal headings; record nulls if evidence is missing, not fictitious headings. Row label alone is not enough for Cox: endpoint + cohort + univariable/multivariable + matched/unmatched + covariate level are necessary to distinguish results. Verify per-source counts and no duplicated P IDs before stage 06. Verify EACH P's endpoint, population, model and cohort label against its anchored source block (including ALL rows before and after each source heading boundary). A heading-to-label mismatch is a hard semantic failure, not a warning. For Cox tables, persist the parent covariate family and reference comparison separately so a row labeled `Yes vs. No` cannot be assigned to the preceding family's parent. Do not propagate any unverified label into canonical results or G groups.

STAGE 06 — ACTUAL SEMANTIC RECONCILIATION (SEPARATE PASS)
Read the frozen Stage 05 records as INPUTS; do NOT redo extraction in this stage. First reconcile inside each table, endpoint, model and population block; then cross-block and cross-source. Assign canonical R00001... only when outcome, contrast/predictor, cohort, analytic method, model, adjustment and source evidence support the proposed equivalence. A report appearing in figure, text, and table may be one R; distinct Cox coefficient rows are separate R even if all report p<0.001. Do not silently merge dissimilar timepoint, adjusted/unadjusted, matched/unmatched, subgroups, or operationalizations. Track confidence and explicit reasons. Keep a mutually exclusive conflict taxonomy: (a) source_format_disagreement; (b) same_test_reporting_discrepancy; (c) possibly_distinct_tests; (d) insufficient_source_label; (e) unassessed. A reporting discrepancy and an unresolved link are different concepts: provide an independent conflict ledger even when a link can be established. For EACH P ID output occurrence_to_result.json with exactly one row containing occurrence_id, result_id or null, disposition (new_test/repeated_report/unresolved), reason, evidence, confidence. Every P ID must be mapped or explicitly unresolved. Generate canonical results Table A named `reconciled_results` with result_id,p,group,source,role,result and Table B named `analysis_groups` with group,outcome,role,structure,adjustment_context. Provide sources of R↔P crosslinks and partial coverage. For uncertain survival summaries (median vs landmark rates), never blanket-merge based solely on repeated p expression. In the second pass, actively search abstract, narrative, captions, tables AND supplements for matching tests; do not leave the entire narrative unmatched as a default. Each narrative P must receive a candidate list with evidence, a positive/negative equivalence decision, or a specific ambiguity. Cross-source equivalence requires outcome, contrast, population, estimator/test, adjustment, direction, and numerical estimate/CI compatibility; identical p-values alone are NEVER enough. Different printed p forms for an otherwise demonstrably same analysis (e.g., P<0.001 versus P=0.001) produce a separate `reporting_discrepancy` record and preserve both raw strings; do not merge by p equality or claim a typo. For potentially distinct analysis variants (e.g., median OS and 5-year OS), determine whether the Methods specify separate timepoint tests versus one global log-rank test reported on multiple summary rows. If one global test is documented, repeat reports point to ONE R; if distinct tests are documented, retain separate Rs. If undecidable, explicitly mark `test_identity_uncertain` and withhold the final distinct total. Baseline mean and median summaries with identical comparative p-values are candidates for the SAME comparison, subject to confirmation of the test used. Record all candidate links, rejected alternatives and audit evidence in `reconciliation/cross_source_candidate_links.json`. Reconciliation MUST be a fresh pass over frozen Stage 05 data, never trivial first-pass 1:1 reassignments.


STAGE 06B — COVERAGE, REUSE, AND TEST-IDENTITY AUDIT
For EVERY narrative/abstract p-value, evaluate possible matches among all source tables and classify `matched`, `same_test_conflicting_report`, `distinct_test`, or `unresolved`. A blanket `unresolved` for a large narrative subset is a FAILED audit, not satisfactory conservative reconciliation. Verify repeated labels with text-level evidence, not repeated p thresholds. Check ALL Kaplan–Meier summary tables for repeated survival-comparison p-values across median and year-specific summaries and verify whether a global log-rank test was merely repeated. Do not turn the same log-rank test into multiple canonical Rs because it appears on 5-, 10- and 15-year summary rows. Record a separate count of actual tests, estimate reports, summary rows and landmarks. Check potential same-test comparisons expressed in both median and mean descriptions. For each P, record candidate comparator IDs, evidence for match/rejection, p-string compatibility, decision and confidence; issue a machine-readable audit (not just a prose note).

STAGE 07 — METHODS, ENDPOINTS, FLEXIBILITY AND MULTIPLICITY
Read Methods, Results and all supplements. Determine trial registration separately from whether authors explicitly state prospective registration; never infer timing from NCT prefix. Distinguish designated primary clinical outcome from exact primary hypothesis TEST. Enumerate distinct O outcomes, statistical model TEMPLATES M, fitted applications, P populations, tested T timepoints, exploratory/sensitivity/subgroup analyses, formal multiplicity F families ONLY where explicitly supported. Record Q broad question and H formal hypotheses when reported. Classify potential repeated-model flexibility without alleging p-hacking. CI and effect estimates must link to distinct R items when possible. Preserve correlations/clustering and analysis-unit caveats. Report source-grounded/unknown rather than fill every category with FALSE. Extract full byline identity records (all authors, affiliations by printed markers, corresponding emails only if printed; last author is not automatically senior) and AI disclosures. Generate model_runs/<run>/inferential_structure_inventory.json and methods_flags.json with evidence per proposition. Counts of applications, tests, and model templates must remain distinct.


STAGE 07B — ARTICLE-LEVEL CLASSIFICATION EVIDENCE (MANDATORY; 111 CANONICAL FIELDS UNCHANGED)
For the scientific-methods and inferential-structure fields, write an auditable SOURCE-TO-CLASSIFICATION evidence inventory, not a private reasoning transcript. Record the concise original quoted phrase when present (or accurately labeled paraphrase), exact article/supplement page and heading/table locator, the observable facts, an explicit short classification rule, uncertainty/counterevidence, and whether the classification is explicit, derived, inferred, negative after scoped inspection, or unresolved. A bare TRUE/FALSE/count without source-grounded justification is insufficient. Search main Methods, Results, applicable tables, figures and ALL relevant supplements. Record author terminology separately from researcher taxonomy: e.g., 'confirmatory propensity score-matched analysis' might support a broader robustness/sensitivity classification INFERRED, but cannot be misquoted as the authors expressly calling it a 'sensitivity analysis'. A negative high_dimensional_screen must describe the inspected sections and the absence of a systematic broad predictor/feature screen, NOT 'not found' without inspecting the applicable sources. Use NA, not FALSE/0, when coverage is incomplete. Never invent verbatim quotations, exact page numbers, literal terms, or clinical primary endpoints.

REQUIRED FIELDS with individual audit entries: study_design, primary_endpoint_declared, primary_outcome_prespecified, exploratory_analysis_declared, analysis_plan_prespecified, multiple_outcomes, n_distinct_outcomes, subgroup_analyses, n_subgroups_tested, high_dimensional_screen, repeated_model_family, sensitivity_analyses, supplement_contains_sensitivity_analyses, n_model_specifications, n_timepoints_tested, adjusted_model_reported, mentions_multiple_testing, multiplicity_method, supplement_contains_model_grids, supplement_materially_changes_qc. Also include any other contested scientific interpretation or quantitative count with an explanation, especially when TRUE, FALSE, or a positive integer drives downstream research.

The evidence for an inferred TRUE must include an observable method/result and the rule that qualifies it. For FALSE supply an inspection scope and specific exclusion rule; NA records a missing-source/ambiguity reason. Counts must be recoverable from an itemized inventory, not just asserted. Distinct outcomes: include each counted outcome's operational label, time origin, event/measurement definition, analysis population, source locator and rationale separating it from other outcomes; 5-/10-/15-year *descriptive summaries* do not create additional outcomes. If n_subgroups_tested or n_model_specifications is a numeric count, provide itemized populations/model templates in the evidence/inventory, distinguishing models from coefficient rows. Record exact words 'not explicitly called sensitivity' when relevant.

Store machine-readable `model_runs/<run>/article_level_evidence.json` as `{ "schema":"article_evidence_v1.15.1", "article_id":"...", "fields":[ {field,value,basis,source_locator,source_evidence,classification_rule,caveat}, ... ] }` and `model_runs/<run>/operational_outcomes.json` as `{ "schema":"operational_outcomes_v1.15.1", "article_id":"...", "outcomes":[ {outcome_id,outcome_label,time_origin,event_definition,population,source_locator,counted}, ... ] }`, with the same records as the reviewer-facing R file. Do not duplicate extraction or alter frozen P/R/G identities to satisfy the evidence tables. If time is limited, preserve the populated reconciliation and write partial evidence with explicit 'unresolved' labels; don't discard scientifically supported Tables A/B to satisfy evidence reporting.

STAGE 08 — HUMAN-READABLE ARTICLE R GENERATION
At top: article header with FIRST AUTHOR, title, year, volume/issue, canonical PII, DOI, status, concise P count/dashboard by table and source. Report n_printed_occurrences, n_mapped_occurrences, n_unresolved_occurrences, n_canonical_results_known. If incomplete, n_unique_results must be NA_character_ (exact schema 2.8 field 083 type), never a misleading proposed-total; do not use NA_integer_ for this character-typed field. Print each source vector with ONE p-value per line and short right-hand comment "# P000001 | R00001 or UNRESOLVED | endpoint | model/population | predictor/contrast | source row"; section headers split long tables and retain true source order. No horizontal 1,000-value dumps. Separate abstract, main narrative, numbered main tables, each numbered supplemental table, numbered figures and threshold annotations. Main and supplement backward-compatible aggregates derive from the component vectors; HTML and PDF renderings cannot double-count.
Then Tables A and B plus concise link/inferential footprint notes. Then exactly one `jaad_qc_article <- tibble::tibble(...)` containing all 111 schema fields below, logical/character/integer/list typed correctly, each with an explicit concise right-hand comment defining its meaning; unknown is typed NA, never invented. A partial Table A and B MUST exist with explicit status even if they contain no reliable results. Generate qc/model_unverified/<PII>_qc.R and qc/human_verified/<PII>_qc.R BYTE IDENTICAL initially. Only the human copy may later be edited. End file with exactly `# jaad_qc_article$human_verified <- TRUE` as the final line. Keep manuscript PDF and complete raw evidence OUTSIDE these reviewer-facing vectors.


MANDATORY ARTICLE-LEVEL EVIDENCE SECTION IN BOTH REVIEWER R FILES: After Tables A and B, IMMEDIATELY BEFORE `jaad_qc_article <- tibble::tibble(...)`, write the heading `# ARTICLE-LEVEL EVIDENCE — source, rule, and uncertainty` and an executable `article_level_evidence <- tibble::tribble(...)`, one concise ONE-LINE ROW PER TARGET FIELD with EXACT columns `field`, `value`, `basis`, `source_locator`, `source_evidence`, `classification_rule`, `caveat`. All seven cells are character strings, including literal `"TRUE"`, `"FALSE"`, `"NA"`, and counts `"6"`; basis MUST be `explicit`, `inferred`, `derived`, `negative_after_inspection`, or `unresolved`. Quote only the author's ACTUAL words when quotation marks used; otherwise prefix source_evidence with `Paraphrase:`. Never claim an exhaustive search if one was not performed. `source_locator` must be actionable (e.g., main PDF page 3 Methods; Supplement Table 7) or explain missing-source limitations. `classification_rule` states the short observable-to-code rationale, not internal deliberation. `caveat` notes absence of literal terminology, limitations, competing interpretations, or `none`. Reflect the current 111-field value; if it changes, update the evidence row, and vice versa. All 20 mandatory fields in Stage 07B must appear exactly once, even when value is NA; add rows for additional noteworthy judgments.

Immediately below that, write `operational_outcomes <- tibble::tribble(...)` with columns `outcome_id`, `outcome_label`, `time_origin`, `event_definition`, `population`, `source_locator`, `counted`; one row per operational outcome (and separate descriptive-only entries if relevant with counted="no"). The integer n_distinct_outcomes, when known, MUST equal the number of `counted="yes"` entries. Distinguish endpoints by definition and analysis time origin, not p-value or repeated survival landmark alone. If a source supports no reliable outcome rows, still write an empty typed tibble with these seven columns and use NA count; do not populate fictitious outcomes. Optionally include `tested_subgroups <- tibble::tribble(...)` and `model_template_inventory <- tibble::tribble(...)` when their numeric counts are asserted; item counts must agree. These two human-readable tables DO NOT change 111 fields or Tables A/B. Use one concise summary per variable and keep full evidence/source locators in archived JSON when necessary.

Keep these R objects executable and easy to read; avoid long narrative essays, deeply nested lists, and static comments masquerading as an evidence table. Make row columns concise enough for human editing. Place the field's same-line definition in jaad_qc_article and add `# see article_level_evidence` where especially helpful. Do not modify existing P vectors, Table A/B, R/G IDs, or verified status logic. The model_unverified and human_verified files must remain initially byte-identical and end with the unchanged commented human_verified line.

STAGE 09 — EXECUTABLE VALIDATION, PACKAGE, AND MANIFEST
Validate independently with available Python and R. Check the 20 required article-level evidence rows are unique and match each 111-field article annotation, source locators are present for explicit/inferred/derived claims, negative decisions name inspection scope, operational outcome inventory counts match n_distinct_outcomes when given, and the evidence R objects are syntactically valid. Cross-check corresponding model_runs evidence JSON without claiming semantic verification from structural agreement. Check source-component counts, every P maps exactly once, all P values identical between JSON and R, P/R/G link referential integrity, canonical R distinctness and unresolved state, 111 field names AND types, R syntactic parse if Rscript is actually installed, valid end marker, initial pair byte equality SHA256, published identity, source hashes, required provenance snapshots, and real stage execution statuses. Also validate source_block_id coverage, presence of genuine anchored source headers, P/outcome versus block/outcome compatibility, block transition resets, canonical R/group outcome compatibility, narrative cross-source candidate coverage, timepoint-vs-global-test classification, and existence of conflict ledgers. Unknown/unanchored evidence or identity ambiguities MUST block complete status. Validator must reconstruct counts FROM rows; dashboards and named stage-status files do not themselves constitute evidence that stages ran. Run the validator on files actually written, not inferred in memory. If possible, audit one sample per block and all transition-adjacent rows against the original PDF. If a local named validator is unavailable in ChatGPT, implement equivalent checks directly; DO NOT pretend to run a tool you lack. Store reports/preflight_validation.json with per-check pass/fail/skipped/unknown. Mark PARTIAL if a material gate fails or semantic/source evidence incomplete; still deliver partial ZIP and review checklist. Archive all genuine intermediate outputs, source discrepancy ledgers and frozen stage JSON (01–09) under model_runs/<run>/. Preserve historical source transformations in provenance, no reconstructed claims. Output a downloadable ZIP containing qc/, model_runs/, reconciliation/, source_reconciliation/, run_provenance/, reports/, README_review.md, and source inventory hashes. At end of response provide factual counts and exact blockers. Do not ask user to manually repeat this startup prompt.


COMPLETENESS GATES AND REPRODUCIBILITY RULES (MANDATORY)
G1 Source inventory and each independent extraction exist (if both formats were available).
G2 Every source p value has an independently anchored BLOCK ID/heading, or unresolved is transparently declared; source-heading transitions, including across tables and page breaks, pass.
G3 Every P is mapped once; no canonical R or group label contradicts the anchored block; all matched narrative reports have positive source evidence.
G4 Survival p reuses and median/landmark distinctions are audited explicitly, not silently treated as tests; conflicts are classified and preserved even after linking.
G5 All 20 required article-level evidence mappings, operational outcome inventory counts, plus all 111 CANONICAL fields/types (including character-typed field 083), R vectors, initial R-byte equality and real validation states pass.
G6 The run output is audited with a separately written validation routine when possible, and any source-specific replay fixtures are stored separately from THIS generalized prompt. Never hard-code expected counts, paper-specific article IDs, or reference outcomes into the general workflow.
A run with failed/unknown material gates is PARTIAL; n_unique_results remains typed NA. The provisional candidate R count is not an independently validated unique test count and MUST NOT be presented as such. Identical counts across runs do not establish semantic reproducibility: compute per-source and per-test crosslinks using source locators, outcome/contrast and model semantics. NEVER claim a prior run, full package replacement, or automated R checking was performed unless it truly was.

REVIEWER CLASSIFICATION RULES
- Boolean TRUE=source-positive; FALSE=source-negative after adequate inspection; NA=not sufficiently assessed. Never infer an affirmative from model convention alone.
- P values are printed character strings retaining <, >, = operators; do not silently turn <0.001 into exact 0.001.
- Reconciled results count is number of unique supported tests, NOT number of printed rows, outcome families, timepoints, covariate values, or model configurations.
- "Significant" means under the source-defined threshold and applicable adjustment; nominal significance is distinct. If threshold/adjustment not knowable mark unknown.
- Explicitly flag conflicts between source formats, between narrative and table, and within supplements (e.g. nearby survival summaries displaying different p-values) without resolving by guess.
- No results or methods from unrelated articles may be imported or used as defaults. Never refer to any demonstration article as required context.

