## 2026-10-15 {.oi-entry-date #2026-10-15}

<details class="oi-callout oi-day-accordion" data-oi-day="2026-10-15">
<summary><span class="oi-day-date">October 15, 2026</span></summary>
<div class="oi-day-body">
<details class="oi-callout oi-log-entry oi-milestone">
<summary><span class="oi-callout-title">Scientific model reconstruction, DAG methodology, author claims, and redesign of human QC</span></summary>
<div class="oi-callout-body">


### Research direction: from p-value extraction to the anatomy of published inference {.unlisted}

**October 15 was a major conceptual and engineering development day.** The project's target is now a **source-grounded, relational description of how each paper frames its scientific questions, defines outcomes, selects and fits statistical models, reports its tests and effect estimates, and supports its stated conclusions**. The p-value occurrence registry (P), reconciled statistical results (R), analysis groups (G), outcomes (O), and scientific questions (Q) remain central, but are no longer sufficient by themselves. The emphasis is on an interpretable *inferential footprint*, not an article-level p-value count or a speculative p-hacking score.

Three complementary directions were prioritized: **reconstruct published regression models**, document **covariate selection and reported DAG-related methods**, and link **major author claims to their supporting or qualifying evidence**. Causal adequacy, source-fidelity adjudication, and author-claim concordance must remain separate from routine data extraction and from a reviewer confirming that a published table was transcribed correctly.

### Model reconstruction and covariate selection: review the equation, not just a list of coefficients {.unlisted}

The key design decision was to represent a reported model in **human-readable R formula notation**, with its outcome and predictor terms visible together—for example, a *proposed* survival specification of the form `survival::Surv(time, event) ~ primary_site + age + breslow_thickness + ulceration`. This is a **representation reconstructed from the manuscript and regression tables**, **not** the authors' recovered code and **not** a refitted analysis. Logistic, linear, Cox, Poisson, and other families need the corresponding model family, link/offset, sample restriction, outcome/time origin, interactions, stratification or clustering when documented.

The scientific records distinguish **each fitted specification** (MOD) from its **model-specific predictor terms** (TERM/CV), the associated **univariable candidate models**, and the individual **coefficient-level R tests**. A model table containing many coefficients is not necessarily many fitted models; conversely, an outcome/analysis group may contain several model variants. Record predictor *included*, *screened candidate*, *explicitly excluded*, or *unresolved*, with source locators and timing where stated. Absence from an adjusted table does **not** independently prove that a candidate was removed by a significance screen.

**Li et al. (2025) as the methodological example:** the Methods explicitly describe retaining factors with **univariable p < 0.20** for multivariable Cox adjustment. An MSS univariable-versus-multivariable reconstruction from supplemental tables illustrates the intended paired presentation. The equation and the full adjustment set must be reviewed against each printed table and its footnotes before human confirmation; coding, missing variables, model populations, and time origins may remain unresolved. The proposed reconstructions are **not human-verified**.

### DAG-related scientific data and a future expert causal audit {.unlisted}

The extraction layer should preserve *what investigators report*: selection strategies (univariable screening, prespecified clinical covariates, stepwise/penalized procedures, etc.), whether a **DAG is reported**, whether it is **actually used to choose covariates**, and whether an adjustment set or estimand is documented. These are distinct observations. A reported multivariable model does not establish causal intent or justify labeling individual terms as confounders.

A separate, later study can have clinical/statistical experts formulate **explicit causal questions, plausible DAGs, and admissible adjustment sets** and compare them with the human-confirmed published models. This could identify *potential* omitted confounders, mediator adjustment, collider conditioning, or temporally inappropriate adjustment—but all depend on the estimand, cohort selection, measurement timing, and substantive assumptions. Without individual-level data or defensible identification assumptions, these comparisons do **not** directly quantify the bias of the published estimates. The intensive DAG adjudication belongs in a selected expert-reviewed subset, not as automatic labels for every paper.

### Author claims and evidence concordance {.unlisted}

Added a separate conceptual layer for a **small number of consequential author conclusions** (C), retaining exact quotation where feasible, manuscript location, qualifications, and links to relevant Q/O/G/R evidence—including contrary or qualifying results. Claims are **not** identical to scientific questions, regression coefficients, or significant p-values. A new extraction starts with claims **`not_assessed`** for evidence-concordance judgment; expert review must consider effect sizes, CIs, design, multiplicity, robustness and wording before assigning an alignment classification. A non-significant p-value by itself does not establish equivalence or absence of effect.

### October 15 engineering sequence and architectural consolidation {.unlisted}

- **v1.16 / schema 2.10:** formal model-building/DAG decisions, author claims, claim–evidence links and separate claim assessments were added to the prior relational design. Human confirmation remained distinct from scientific correctness.
- **v1.16.1:** grouped scientific and source-level checkpoints advanced the idea of *reviewing one meaningful unit at a time*, rather than uncommenting a verification line for each individual p-value. The required convention is one `# qc_confirm("GROUP_ID")` per complete reviewable unit.
- **v1.17 / schema 2.11:** introduced explicit reconstructed model specifications and model-specific covariates. Reviewer-facing equations became a first-class requirement. This release described **215 defined field positions across 13 tables** (including the historical 111-field article-summary table); those numbers were schema column definitions, not 215 independent article-level observations.
- **Schema 3.0 conceptual reset:** the older 111-field table ceased to be the organizing unit. The proposed unified ontology now comprises **23 linked tables across seven scientific domains**, with **269 defined field positions**, inclusive of keys and provenance. A complete numbered field dictionary was appended for human readability. This is an architectural definition, **not evidence that every table was scientifically populated correctly in a real paper**.
- **Operational application decision:** preserve the existing Shiny dashboard, article catalog, article source gathering, and ChatGPT upload workflow. Replace its packaged `START_HERE_ChatGPT.txt` instructions; do **not** substitute a new lightweight dashboard or change article-selection behavior. The schema 3.0 stand-alone extraction contract is a proposed new output specification; older local dashboard validators still need compatibility work before they can certify those outputs.

### Human-review QMD: major usability regression discovered, not resolved {.unlisted}

The target workflow was clarified repeatedly: **one authoritative, self-contained, editable Quarto `.qmd`**, with a **collapsible section for each meaningful source, scientific table or model**, the **complete human-readable R record inside that section**, and the section's own `# qc_confirm("...")` at the bottom. The source locator, result labels, printed p values, model formula, adjustment terms and unresolved issues should be visible in that same unit. The actual Quarto HTML should be **full viewport width**, use the established OIP styling, and have a genuinely useful table of contents with individual review-unit links. A separate read-only HTML preview may be convenient but cannot substitute for a functional QMD.

**Real Li pilot failure (end-of-day):** the generated output instead split scientific data into `article_data.R` (~1 MB) and `article_review.qmd`, with the latter calling `source("article_data.R", local = TRUE)` and typically printing only a named tibble followed by a confirmation comment. R abbreviated wide tibbles (e.g., “7 more variables”), and a small number of extremely long column-vector lines in the R file made human editing impractical. The QMD's high-level table of contents did not navigate to individual review sections; source review showed inventory metadata rather than sufficiently accessible p-value evidence. `qc_confirm()` was not defined, so uncommenting it could fail. The attractive standalone HTML preview and actual Quarto-rendered HTML were **different interfaces**: good preview styling did not demonstrate that the real QMD was usable. The displayed model formula was not reliably synchronized to the canonical model table. **This was judged an unacceptable regression and is NOT a completed human-QC implementation.**

The pilot Li extract was explicitly **PARTIAL, model-unverified and human-QC pending**. Its displayed provisional counts included **1,047 printed p occurrences**, **996 proposed result records**, **32 model records**, and **16 open audit items**; these are run-specific *unadjudicated* quantities, not a certified gold standard. They should not be silently reconciled with earlier Li runs reporting 994 proposed results. Software/schema checks and synthetic previews are not substitutes for a real-paper, source-level comparison or reviewer usability test.

### Decisions and next-session acceptance test {.unlisted}

1. **Do not redesign the Shiny app again.** Keep the established article-selection → source-package → `START_HERE` → ChatGPT → output-ZIP process. Update the extraction/review instruction contract, not the entry point.
2. **Replace the human-review contract** with exactly one editable scientific `.qmd` as the primary review artifact. Put each meaningful R data definition, actual per-row values and source annotations *inside its own code chunk/panel*; JSON can remain the machine-readable research archive. A separate `.R` should not be required to edit or verify scientific entries.
3. Put **one working confirmation function** in the QMD and one initially commented `qc_confirm()` line **at the end of each reviewable chunk**. The frozen model proposal, human edits, open exceptions, and final verification gates must remain independently traceable. Merely expanding a panel or rendering HTML never counts as confirmation.
4. Verify the **actual rendered QMD**, not just the standalone preview: full-width responsive layout, OIP CSS available, clear individual-unit TOC, all relevant field values/rows visible, no horizontally unmanageable mega-lines, and self-contained chunk-by-chunk editing. Test with **Li's very large supplement** and a smaller contrasting article; retain the distinction between syntax/unit tests, live R/Quarto execution and source-scientific adjudication.
5. Preserve the current schema 3.0 field dictionary and source-level methodology while checking that the interface is usable. **Stop calling the updated workflow production ready until a complete real-article QMD passes the investigator's review.**

**Research status at close of October 15:** substantial progress in ontology and scientific aims; **human-review presentation remains an open engineering blocker**. The proper next step is a faithful single-QMD pilot, not another wholesale architecture change.


