Build exactly one stable P000001... entry per canonical logical source occurrence in labeled_occurrences.json with fields: occurrence_id, source_component, source_order, reported_p, result_label, outcome, predictor_or_contrast, population, analysis_method, model_specification, adjustment_context, table_section, source_location, evidence_text, label_status (supported/partial/unresolved), plus optional source_variant_matches, regression_covariate_family, reference_level, effect_estimate, confidence_interval. Every R-vector p must refer to a registry P ID; preserve ambiguity explicitly. Each P entry MUST additionally carry source_block_id, heading_anchor_text, endpoint_anchor_status, and label_origin (heading/table row/narrative/inferred/unknown), with source-literal headings; record nulls if evidence is missing, not fictitious headings. Row label alone is not enough for Cox: endpoint + cohort + univariable/multivariable + matched/unmatched + covariate level are necessary to distinguish results. Verify per-source counts and no duplicated P IDs before stage 06. Verify EACH P's endpoint, population, model and cohort label against its anchored source block (including ALL rows before and after each source heading boundary). A heading-to-label mismatch is a hard semantic failure, not a warning. For Cox tables, persist the parent covariate family and reference comparison separately so a row labeled `Yes vs. No` cannot be assigned to the preceding family's parent. Do not propagate any unverified label into canonical results or G groups.

STAGE 06 — ACTUAL SEMANTIC RECONCILIATION (SEPARATE PASS)
Read the frozen Stage 05 records as INPUTS; do NOT redo extraction in this stage. First reconcile inside each table, endpoint, model and population block; then cross-block and cross-source. Assign canonical R00001... only when outcome, contrast/predictor, cohort, analytic method, model, adjustment and source evidence support the proposed equivalence. A report appearing in figure, text, and table may be one R; distinct Cox coefficient rows are separate R even if all report p<0.001. Do not silently merge dissimilar timepoint, adjusted/unadjusted, matched/unmatched, subgroups, or operationalizations. Track confidence and explicit reasons. Keep a mutually exclusive conflict taxonomy: (a) source_format_disagreement; (b) same_test_reporting_discrepancy; (c) possibly_distinct_tests; (d) insufficient_source_label; (e) unassessed. A reporting discrepancy and an unresolved link are different concepts: provide an independent conflict ledger even when a link can be established. For EACH P ID output occurrence_to_result.json with exactly one row containing occurrence_id, result_id or null, disposition (new_test/repeated_report/unresolved), reason, evidence, confidence. Every P ID must be mapped or explicitly unresolved. Generate canonical results Table A named `reconciled_results` with result_id,p,group,source,role,result and Table B named `analysis_groups` with group,outcome,role,structure,adjustment_context. Provide sources of R↔P crosslinks and partial coverage. For uncertain survival summaries (median vs landmark rates), never blanket-merge based solely on repeated p expression. In the second pass, actively search abstract, narrative, captions, tables AND supplements for matching tests; do not leave the entire narrative unmatched as a default. Each narrative P must receive a candidate list with evidence, a positive/negative equivalence decision, or a specific ambiguity. Cross-source equivalence requires outcome, contrast, population, estimator/test, adjustment, direction, and numerical estimate/CI compatibility; identical p-values alone are NEVER enough. Different printed p forms for an otherwise demonstrably same analysis (e.g., P<0.001 versus P=0.001) produce a separate `reporting_discrepancy` record and preserve both raw strings; do not merge by p equality or claim a typo. For potentially distinct analysis variants (e.g., median OS and 5-year OS), determine whether the Methods specify separate timepoint tests versus one global log-rank test reported on multiple summary rows. If one global test is documented, repeat reports point to ONE R; if distinct tests are documented, retain separate Rs. If undecidable, explicitly mark `test_identity_uncertain` and withhold the final distinct total. Baseline mean and median summaries with identical comparative p-values are candidates for the SAME comparison, subject to confirmation of the test used. Record all candidate links, rejected alternatives and audit evidence in `reconciliation/cross_source_candidate_links.json`. Reconciliation MUST be a fresh pass over frozen Stage 05 data, never trivial first-pass 1:1 reassignments.


STAGE 06B — COVERAGE, REUSE, AND TEST-IDENTITY AUDIT
For EVERY narrative/abstract p-value, evaluate possible matches among all source tables and classify `matched`, `same_test_conflicting_report`, `distinct_test`, or `unresolved`. A blanket `unresolved` for a large narrative subset is a FAILED audit, not satisfactory conservative reconciliation. Verify repeated labels with text-level evidence, not repeated p thresholds. Check ALL Kaplan–Meier summary tables for repeated survival-comparison p-values across median and year-specific summaries and verify whether a global log-rank test was merely repeated. Do not turn the same log-rank test into multiple canonical Rs because it appears on 5-, 10- and 15-year summary rows. Record a separate count of actual tests, estimate reports, summary rows and landmarks. Check potential same-test comparisons expressed in both median and mean descriptions. For each P, record candidate comparator IDs, evidence for match/rejection, p-string compatibility, decision and confidence; issue a machine-readable audit (not just a prose note).

STAGE 07 — METHODS, ENDPOINTS, FLEXIBILITY AND MULTIPLICITY
Read Methods, Results and all supplements. Determine trial registration separately from whether authors explicitly state prospective registration; never infer timing from NCT prefix. Distinguish designated primary clinical outcome from exact primary hypothesis TEST. Enumerate distinct O outcomes, statistical model TEMPLATES M, fitted applications, P populations, tested T timepoints, exploratory/sensitivity/subgroup analyses, formal multiplicity F families ONLY where explicitly supported. Record Q broad question and H formal hypotheses when reported. Classify potential repeated-model flexibility without alleging p-hacking. CI and effect estimates must link to distinct R items when possible. Preserve correlations/clustering and analysis-unit caveats. Report source-grounded/unknown rather than fill every category with FALSE. Extract full byline identity records (all authors, affiliations by printed markers, corresponding emails only if printed; last author is not automatically senior) and AI disclosures. Generate model_runs/<run>/inferential_structure_inventory.json and methods_flags.json with evidence per proposition. Counts of applications, tests, and model templates must remain distinct.


STAGE 07B — ARTICLE-LEVEL CLASSIFICATION EVIDENCE (MANDATORY; 111 CANONICAL FIELDS UNCHANGED)
For the scientific-methods and inferential-structure fields, write an auditable SOURCE-TO-CLASSIFICATION evidence inventory, not a private reasoning transcript. Record the concise original quoted phrase when present (or accurately labeled paraphrase), exact article/supplement page and heading/table locator, the observable facts, an explicit short classification rule, uncertainty/counterevidence, and whether the classification is explicit, derived, inferred, negative after scoped inspection, or unresolved. A bare TRUE/FALSE/count without source-grounded justification is insufficient. Search main Methods, Results, applicable tables, figures and ALL relevant supplements. Record author terminology separately from researcher taxonomy: e.g., 'confirmatory propensity score-matched analysis' might support a broader robustness/sensitivity classification INFERRED, but cannot be misquoted as the authors expressly calling it a 'sensitivity analysis'. A negative high_dimensional_screen must describe the inspected sections and the absence of a systematic broad predictor/feature screen, NOT 'not found' without inspecting the applicable sources. Use NA, not FALSE/0, when coverage is incomplete. Never invent verbatim quotations, exact page numbers, literal terms, or clinical primary endpoints.

