### Today's research and engineering decisions {.unlisted}

**Scientific objective clarified.** The first Open Inference paper examines the reported *inferential footprint* of contemporary dermatologic research: frequency of printed p-values and distinct statistical results; frequentist versus Bayesian methods; design and primary-endpoint declarations; prespecification/registration timing; multiplicity; alpha/sidedness; and confidence-interval reporting. Sample-size/power details, effect-size characterization, AI disclosures and richer claims/model structures remain scientifically valuable, but are **not prerequisites** for the first paper's human-QC completion. Article identity and authorship remain available for later checking. Never let the fuller 23-table research schema become a reason to postpone publication-one QC.

**Adopted architecture.** The existing Shiny article-selection → prepare/upload sources and current `START_HERE_ChatGPT.txt` → ChatGPT extraction → output ZIP workflow remains. All **23 scientific tables** in schema **3.0.0** (seven domains, **269 column definitions**, not 269 independent study variables) should still be proposed in the backend. Six paired independent R scripts, with matching names in `qc/model_unverified/` and `qc/human_verified/`, are generated up front:

| Review module | Primary material | Human verification priority |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `01_results_qc.R` | All printed P occurrences and semantic P→R reconciliation, display-first R-centered `tibble::tribble()` | **First manuscript** |
| `02_inferential_reporting_qc.R` | Study design, frequentist/Bayesian methods, registration, primary endpoint/test, multiplicity, thresholds and CI reporting | **First manuscript** |
| `03_additional_reporting_qc.R` | Bibliography/authorship, subject counts and power, effect estimates, AI, funding and conflicts | Deferred, independent |
| `04_scientific_structure_qc.R` | Questions, operational outcomes, groups, sensitivity/subgroup families | Deferred |
| `05_models_dag_qc.R` | Model specification/terms, selection, adjustment and DAG decisions | Deferred |
| `06_claims_evidence_qc.R` | Author claims, linked evidence, qualifications | Deferred |

**Preserve Module 01's reviewer-facing design.** Each publication table/figure receives one compact editable `tribble`, in the order it appears in the *main paper* and then *supplements*, followed by narrative-only results. Each row is a **proposed distinct R**, with `r_id`, printed `p_value`, associated `p_ids`, source-like `result_label`, and **editable** `other_printings` and `other_locations` (not comments). A separately commented `# qc_confirm("...")` beneath each display means human confirmation **only after source inspection**; none are preconfirmed. An unchanged duplicate printing belongs to the same statistical R *only when scientific identity is supported*; identical p-values alone never establish equivalence. The original model files are frozen; human edits must not silently imply that the backend has been corrected when an exporter has not actually run.

### Measured extraction experiments and what they established {.unlisted}

**Li et al. high-density stress test (PII `S0190962224027683`).** One six-module run reported **927 P occurrences** and **903 proposed R results** in ~16 minutes; internal mapping checks passed, yet the source extraction was explicitly partial. A source-level comparison with an earlier 1,047-location run revealed a **120-occurrence deficit**: 11 from omitted main Table III, 28 in abstract/narrative, and 81 supplemental table p-values concentrated near the top of **38 continuation pages**. These figures describe the comparison of model runs, not a human-certified truth set. The specific failure motivated mandatory page-level coverage checks; simple referential integrity between *already extracted* P/R records cannot detect omitted source evidence.

**Source-coverage and coordinate test.** After revising START_HERE, a further Li extraction (~**14 min 38 sec**) reported **35 main-PDF table p-values** (all 35 matched in main HTML), **976 supplemental-PDF occurrences**, and **36 additional main-PDF narrative/figure occurrences**: **1,047 canonical PDF-derived P observations** in total. The **28 HTML narrative observations** were separately flagged as unresolved; they must **not** simply be added to the PDF total without logical-source matching. The supplemental PDF has no corresponding HTML source, so main HTML vs main PDF is the relevant cross-format comparison, while the supplement uses page-by-page PDF audits. These are run-reported, provisional counts, not human QC outcomes.

**Measured PDF geometry returned.** New `source_reconciliation/p_pdf_coordinates.json` records source filename and hash, physical PDF page/index, page size and rotation, literal text, P/observation IDs, bounding box `[x0,y0,x1,y1]` in **PDF points from the top-left origin**, extraction method and coordinate status. The accompanying `reports/p_coordinate_coverage.json` breaks down counts page by page. The run reported **66/71 main-PDF** and **976/976 supplemental-PDF** observations with measured coordinates (**1,042/1,047** overall); five main-PDF locations remained unavailable, not fabricated. Sampled native-PDF coordinates landed on their expected printed values; that is stronger provenance than page-only locators, but it does **not** establish that all statistical R identities are correct.

**Critical remaining inference limitation.** The same run proposed **1,011 R records**—exactly `35 main table + 976 supplemental`, each initially linked to a single P—with **36 additional main-PDF P identities unresolved** and an independently flagged HTML-narrative reconciliation. Module 01 had largely empty `other_printings`, so the P→R deduplication **had not genuinely occurred**, despite strong P discovery. Figure-caption PDF text also sometimes carried extraction artifacts such as `P \\.001`. A high total of R values is not, by itself, proof of statistical distinctness. A complete printed inventory and a reconciled distinct-test inventory are different deliverables.

