   R/P IDs examined (or explicit none), identity dimensions used, disposition
   (`same_test`, `distinct_test`, `unresolved_identity`), assigned `r_id` or null,
   evidence locator(s)/footnotes and concise decision rationale, plus any open
   issue/discrepancy IDs. Every repeated-report link needs positive source evidence;
   every rejected plausible link needs a concrete differentiating dimension.
   Additionally write `source_reconciliation/p_to_r_candidate_pairs.json` for
   evaluated plausible pairs with accepted/rejected/uncertain decision and the
   supporting/contradicting dimensions. Record only real evaluated comparisons;
   do not invent pair assessments. Keep reporting discrepancies in a separate
   independent ledger so a discrepancy is not confused with uncertainty about
   whether two printings represent the same test. These are AUDIT artifacts,
   not extra tables or editable human scripts in the frozen 23-table schema.

F. BUILD DISTINCT R ONLY FROM THOSE DECISIONS.
   Group P IDs into one R only after establishing statistical identity. Keep
   canonical R IDs stable if an existing assignment can be retained without
   collision; maintain a machine-readable alias/change ledger if a regrouping
   requires different R IDs. Never reassign P IDs, discard coordinates, or
   promote an unresolved equivalence to a new confidently distinct R to make
   the row counts match. Populate the existing `data/statistical_results.json`
   and `data/printed_occurrences.json` from the adjudicated mapping; provide a
   complete P-to-R map with explicitly null/unresolved assignments. Generate
   the unchanged six R review modules (especially Module 01's one R-centered,
   display-first tribble row with all linked P IDs and other_printings/locations)
   FROM this one canonical mapping, not via a separate independent re-extraction.

G. MACHINE-CHECK CONSERVATION AND REPORT RECONCILIATION COVERAGE.
   Verify: (1) canonical P IDs remain identical before and after reconciliation;
   (2) exactly one decision record exists per P and each P is either mapped to
   one R or explicitly unresolved; (3) the count of `same_test` + `distinct_test`
   + `unresolved_identity` P dispositions equals the frozen P count; (4) every
   accepted multi-P R has documented identity evidence; (5) each non-null R is
   represented once in Module 01 and its full P set matches `p_ids`, named
   `other_printings` and named `other_locations`; (6) unresolved P remain
   visible in Module 01 and the unresolved ledger; (7) all original source/PDF
   coordinates survive unchanged. Report in `reports/p_to_r_reconciliation.json`:
   frozen P total, candidate-search-coverage total, candidate pairs examined,
   accepted/rejected/unresolved pairs, singleton R count, multi-P R count and
   their P coverage, unresolved-identity P count, reporting-discrepancy count,
   proposed R total and whether the reconciliation gate PASSED, PARTIAL or NOT_RUN.
   Do not require a minimum number of merged R records: zero repeats can be a
   genuine finding, but all-one-to-one mapping without actual candidate search
   evidence does NOT establish completed reconciliation. Set certified final
   `n_unique_results` unknown/null when source coverage or identity reconciliation
   is incomplete; retain `n_unique_results_proposed` as provisional if useful.
   Native machine checks may be PASS only if executed on the saved outputs;
   scientific identity and human verification remain separate states.

STAGE C — METHODS, SCIENTIFIC QUESTIONS, AND CLAIMS
8. Capture article characteristics, true unit of observation/randomization/analysis, cohort,
   missing data, registration timing (separate from registration existence), power, primary
