For a substantive display whose repeated reports need adjudication, put the
printed literal in `other_printings` and its location in `other_locations`; both
are EDITABLE data cells with names that exactly match the secondary P IDs. If source printings disagree,
retain both raw values; do not silently choose one. For unknown identity, mark
pending with a distinct P and a source-grounded exception; never represent an
unresolved source as a confidently reconciled R. Do NOT add G, Q, O or MOD IDs to
these quick-review rows; those remain linked in structured machine data / modules
04–06. Keep long result labels short for visual scanning and full texts in JSON.

For datasets with >1,000 P occurrences, still use one row per reconciled R;
organize tables by model/outcome subheadings and keep each section reasonable to
review (typically 15–50 R rows). If a published table has multiple endpoints,
add foldable subsections within that same display, each with its own `qc_confirm`.
No 100,000-character lines, no huge list dumps or text-wrapped one-P-per-20-lines.
Do not produce a separate P-vector plus a separate R-review for the same display.
The human should be able to put the publication's Table 1 beside RStudio and
verify p-value, test label, R identity, and attached other printings in one pass.

MODULE 02 — NOMINAL VS MULTIPLICITY TERMINOLOGY (MANDATORY)
Do NOT use `results_reported_nominal` as a generic synonym for an article with
no reported multiplicity correction. Author wording and interpretation of
adjustment status are DIFFERENT observations. Use clearly labeled reviewable
fields in the Module 02 MULTIPLICITY unit, with one independent pending
`# qc_confirm("METHODS_MULTIPLICITY")` checkpoint:
- `nominal_label_explicit`: TRUE only if the authors actually use "nominal" to
  describe the relevant p-values/results; FALSE only after sufficiently broad
  source inspection establishes no such author labeling; NA if not assessed.
  "Exploratory" by itself is not the word "nominal"; retain any such phrasing
  separately as literal source evidence, never silently convert it.
- `p_values_unadjusted`: TRUE only when supported by explicit unadjusted/
  uncorrected wording or defensible analysis-specific evidence; FALSE when the
  selected p-value family is explicitly adjusted; NA when adjustment status is
  unclear or heterogeneous. NO reported correction does NOT prove unadjusted.
- `multiplicity_reported`: TRUE if the manuscript explicitly describes a
  multiplicity adjustment/control procedure applicable to some hypothesis
  family, FALSE after full inspection finds no PROCEDURE, NA if uncertain.
  An acknowledgement/limitation discussion ALONE is not a reported procedure.
- `correction_method` and `family_definition`: source-grounded methods and
  tested comparison families (NA if unknown/not described); do not invent them.
- Include concise supporting source locator, literal author wording when
  present, applicability/scope (which endpoints, families or analyses), and
  `evidence_basis` (`explicit_author_label`, `explicit_method`,
  `reviewer_interpretation`, `negative_after_inspection`, or `unresolved`).
  If an article mixes adjusted and unadjusted families, make separate small
  source-scoped review rows rather than force a single article-wide Boolean.

SCHEMA 3.0 COMPATIBILITY (NO SILENT FIELD ADDITIONS): the authoritative frozen
`statistical_reporting.reports_nominal_p_values` field ALREADY means explicit
AUTHOR characterization as "nominal" (the Module 02 reviewer label
`nominal_label_explicit` is its clear human-facing alias). The existing
`multiplicity_method` maps only to explicit reported method; the existing
`multiplicity_family_reported` indicates whether authors define a family,
whereas a human-readable `family_definition` records its exact description in
review/evidence notes. `mentions_multiple_testing` measures ANY mention or
multiple-testing discussion and must NOT be silently equated with
`multiplicity_reported` (a PROCEDURE). `multiplicity_acknowledged` records
risk/limitation acknowledgement, not a correction. `p_values_unadjusted`,
`multiplicity_reported` and `family_definition` are supplemental REVIEW
annotations under the current schema; retain them in Module 02 and the
source-grounded evidence/derived-review export as appropriate, but do NOT add
unsupported columns to any of the 23 original scientific JSON tables. A future
formal schema version may add permanent fields. Never invent a successful
export of supplemental fields if the assembler is not implemented; report
human-reviewed export as NOT_RUN when applicable. Human verification remains
separate from model-inferred classification and machine schema checks.

