**User action:** download and unzip the entire Stage 1 result, drag **that complete folder** into its corresponding article workspace, edit its R files *in place*, uncomment only actually reviewed `qc_confirm()` scopes, and move completed files to the **same package's** `Verified/` folder. Then select Stage 2 in Shiny; it discovers the nested package, copies the selected input R scripts **byte-for-byte into the upload**, and does not modify the Stage 1 record. Drag the finished Stage 2 package into the same workspace and review Modules 04–06 there. The final app archive reported this day was `Open_Inference_OIP31_TWO_STAGE_APP_PACKAGE_OWNED_QC_2026-10-17.zip`. Its report stated **174 of 179 tests passed, 2 skipped, 3 legacy all-in-one expectation failures**. The native Mac R/Shiny UI was **not independently executed here**, and prior Mac screenshots documented actual routing errors subsequently addressed in the app source. Do not equate source-code unit-test PASS with confirmed end-to-end operation on the investigator's machine.

### Cross-article stress tests, validation boundaries and next actions {.unlisted}

**Hwang capillaroscopy extraction** was a useful counterexample to Li: an early double-pass attempt stopped at **PARTIAL/FAIL** (main 7-page PDF, 12-page supplement, roughly 40 preliminary statistical signals, independent pass B incomplete; no frozen P inventory or complete scientific R package). Subsequent retry failed. A single monolithic model request was not robust to every article's source formatting, particularly line-wrapped supplement P values. This motivated short, resumable stage contracts and durable checkpoints rather than interpreting Li's occasional successful 40-minute all-in-one output as a universally reliable process. Other article-specific pilot outcomes remain separate and must not be inferred from Li's totals.

**Validation boundaries remain non-negotiable:** (1) original source hashes and source-region/page completeness, including alternate-format HTML; (2) immutable PDF-anchored P freeze; (3) proposed P→R structural reconciliation and human-approved identity preservation; (4) reconstructable analysis context and question relationships; (5) native R parse/source where executable; (6) selected schema 3.1 assembly; (7) explicit scope-wise **human scientific review**. Each can be PASS, FAIL, NOT RUN or PARTIAL independently. A structurally complete R registry, source-coordinate matching, model-generated status report, long elapsed run, or location inside a `Verified/` folder **must never become independent proof of scientific validity**. A native R environment must be used to test generated scripts before claiming executable QC.

**Next steps, in order:** preserve all Li variants with source/run hashes; fix the HTML-table coverage regression without altering valid frozen PDF evidence; enforce the already approved Table I duplicate-identity decision through an article-specific regression fixture; ensure the Stage 2 upload includes the Stage 1 freeze/decision ledgers or clearly declares provenance revalidation NOT RUN; perform controlled 30–40-result human source audits of GPT-6 High, standard GPT-6 highest effort and Astra Pro; evaluate scope navigation and editorial burden; validate actual R parse/source and the 25-table schema compiler; finally test several diverse articles before progressing toward an automated batch of 20–100 articles. An article that has never been human-reviewed can still progress **as model-proposed**, with all pending dependencies preserved, but must never be silently labeled independently verified.

**Day-end decision/status:** **two-stage, package-owned, R-first scientific abstraction adopted as the current experimental OIP 3.1 workflow**. Li's stable machine-reported **1,047 PDF P observations**, later **997 proposed R identities**, and fully indexed scientific interpretation demonstrate feasibility. They do **not** establish the scientifically adjudicated unique-test denominator, full cross-format source completeness, completed schema 3.1 export, or human-verified claim/inferential-footprint conclusions. Model-effort benchmarking and genuine scientific QC are the immediate research priorities.


