   validity or author endorsement of a result. Default assessment not_assessed.
7. Every new dimension has SEPARATE model/human/unknown verification scope.
   All important assertions need evidence_records with exact locator, basis
   and scope of inspection. NA/null is not FALSE; no observed link is NOT
   evidence of absence unless source scope was inspected explicitly.
Count printed P, distinct R, source-block roles, operational-question groups,
Q origin, R-to-Q links and C-to-R claims as different observables. Avoid any
quality score inferred from ratios, significance or the stated/test-entailed gap.

STAGE B — COMPLETE PRINTED P INVENTORY BEFORE TEST IDENTITY
6. For EVERY raw p candidate in each available source stream, explicitly determine
   `accepted`, `excluded_with_source_reason` (e.g. Methods' alpha threshold rather
   than a reported result), or `unresolved`; never silently discard candidate tokens.
   A printed location in the main HTML and its equivalent main PDF location yields
   ONE canonical P, with TWO retained source observations. Abstract/Results/caption
   repetitions at DIFFERENT publication locations each get their own P. An actual
   supplement value is a P even if numerically identical to a main-table value.
7. Freeze `printed_occurrences` with exact original literal, P ID, result label,
   source block and locator before deriving any R count or formatting any review
   script. Every main table, figure/caption and narrative block, and every supplement
   page, must have source observations and an inspection/coverage status (including
   explicitly inspected zero). Retain disagreements and uncertain labels without
   guessing. Do not combine P candidates just because the reported p-value is equal.
8. AFTER completing and freezing the canonical P inventory and source-coverage/coordinate
   audits, execute the separate MANDATORY STATISTICAL-IDENTITY RECONCILIATION GATE below.
   Search for repeated reports within analysis families AND across manuscript narrative,
   figures, tables and supplements. Explicitly assess candidate identity; never treat
   one-R-per-P assignments as sufficient merely because every P has an R link.
   Preserve conflicting printed literals and every original P/source observation.
   If any identities remain unresolved, report provisional R totals, not certified totals.
9. Preserve effect estimates and CIs separately; do not infer causal validity,
   multiplicity adjustment or prespecification without evidence. Calculate counts
   from actual persisted rows and source observations, never anticipated totals.

MANDATORY SOURCE-COVERAGE GATE — BEFORE CANONICAL P / R TOTALS ARE ACCEPTED
The following original per-format source checks remain mandatory IN ADDITION TO
source_pass_A/B and the source-freeze validator. PDF native-word versus layout
scans alone are not a substitute for independent SECOND whole-document coverage.
A. Write `source_reconciliation/p_candidate_observations.json` with one row per
   independently observed source-format candidate: source file/role/format, page,
   block (abstract, main table/figure, narrative, supplement table/page), original
   p literal and operator, source locator, scan pass/method, source excerpt/row
   label, and disposition. For every PDF candidate, include measured token bbox,
   page geometry and extraction status under the PDF coordinate contract below;
   for HTML use a DOM/table/row/cell or passage anchor, not fictitious PDF x/y.
   Link matched HTML/PDF depictions rather than discarding either observation.
B. Write `reports/p_source_coverage.json` AND `reports/p_coverage_by_page.csv`.
   Count, for EACH source file independently: raw p candidates, accepted observed
   results, candidates excluded with reason, uncertain candidates, and source
   regions/pages NOT scanned. Break out main HTML tables, HTML prose/captions,
   main PDF tables, PDF prose/captions, AND EVERY supplement separately by page,
   table, block, and original reporting order. The main HTML and PDF subtotals
   are NEVER added together for the canonical total where they depict the same
   locations. Every supplement without an HTML representation is counted from
   its ACTUALLY SUPPLIED representation only. A scan not performed is NOT_RUN,
   never zero; a scanned page with no results is EXPLICIT_ZERO with evidence.
C. Perform TWO distinct coverage passes on EVERY PDF page with a p-bearing
   table (single-page and multipage, including continuations): (1) enumerate
   all literal p expressions via native PDF text/word positions over the ENTIRE
   page, including the first and last rows; (2) extract table-aware cells/labels
   with separate column and page-break context. Reconcile both candidate sets
   by original coordinates and literal. Inspect the top of EVERY supplemental
   continuation page, including values near the top margin (for example within
   the first ~130 PDF points), and the last rows at its bottom. Never start a
   page scan below a fixed header cutoff that discards genuine first data rows.
   Check page-transition endpoint/model and parent-covariate headers. When a
   page cannot be extracted reliably, visually inspect it or mark that page
   UNVERIFIED. If scanned text cannot expose embedded figure/table p values,
   use document/page-image reading and flag any remaining blind spots.
D. PROVE CONSERVATION before report completion: every accepted source-format
   observation either maps to ONE canonical P or is linked as an equivalent
   depiction of that same canonical P; every excluded observation has a grounded
   exclusion reason; each uncertain candidate stays visibly unresolved. Cross-
   check original raw candidate counts against accepted+excluded+unresolved
   dispositions BY PAGE and BY TABLE. Independently audit PDF observation
   coordinate records against observed PDF tokens, with every missing bbox
   accounted for by an explicit status and reason. Check that the P inventory,
   module 01's p_ids, and R-to-P ledger cover the same canonical P ID set.
   A successful
   internal P→R mapping proves only REFERENTIAL integrity, never source coverage.
E. Source coverage must be marked PARTIAL / NOT_RUN unless EVERY source page,
   table, figure/caption, abstract and Results narrative section is accounted for,
   and all unexplained count mismatches or PDF/HTML discrepancies are resolved.
   Stop the source-COMPLETE claim if any numbered table, page, continuation-row
   segment or narrative block was omitted. Still deliver useful PARTIAL data,
   with source-specific outstanding work; don't invent rows or assert completion.
F. In the final extraction handoff, display the compact audit: main HTML observed
   count (and main PDF observed count) separately; each supplemental document's
   observed count; canonical printed P count AFTER cross-format matching; distinct
   proposed R count; unresolved P→R identities; and the exact source-scan status.
   Also show measured/unavailable/not-run PDF coordinate counts BY SOURCE, with
   links to `p_pdf_coordinates.json` and `p_coordinate_coverage.json`. State
   unscanned regions. Compare counts only to THIS run's source evidence,
   not to test papers or historical benchmarks.

MANDATORY PDF COORDINATE PROVENANCE — SOURCE AUDIT, NOT HUMAN REVIEW
This requirement applies to EVERY actual supplied PDF, main and supplemental,
regardless of article or table length. It does not apply to HTML-only content.
Capture physical PDF positions DURING the independent source scan, BEFORE
assigning canonical P IDs or reconciling R identities. Coordinates locate
PRINTED SOURCE OBSERVATIONS, not abstract statistical tests: repeated printings
can have different coordinates even when they resolve to one R.

1. Record a measured p-token bounding box using native PDF character/word geometry
   where extractable (e.g. PyMuPDF/Fitz page.get_text("words"), rawdict, or an
   equivalent installed PDF geometry library). Prefer the bbox of the ORIGINAL
   p expression, including the operator, rather than of the surrounding row,
   table or manually inferred cell. Preserve the exact source literal; if a p
   token spans adjacent words, use the union of the actually matched glyph/word
   boxes, flag that method, and retain the constituent boxes where necessary.
   Do not conflate a table-heading "P" with a reported p-value.
2. Use one explicit coordinate convention per file: page-relative PDF points,
   top-left origin (x increases rightward, y downward). Record coordinate_system,
   page_width_pt, page_height_pt, crop/rotation if applicable, and which geometry
   engine/convention produced the values. Always record BOTH zero-based page_index
   and one-based page_number; printed journal page labels are a distinct optional
   string and must NEVER replace physical PDF page indexing. When rotated/cropped,
   report coordinates in the extraction engine's displayed page frame with its
   transform details; do not silently mix coordinate frames.
3. In `source_reconciliation/p_candidate_observations.json`, assign each
   independently scanned source-format candidate a stable observation_id and
   preserve its exact original source-file/source_id, source SHA-256, raw literal,
   page and locator. For PDF candidates add coordinate_status = MEASURED,
   UNAVAILABLE, or NOT_RUN; when MEASURED record bbox_pt = [x0,y0,x1,y1]
   with x0<=x1, y0<=y1. Never fabricate bounding boxes or report guessed or
   image-relative pixels as PDF points. For HTML candidates, retain the DOM
   element/row/cell/paragraph anchor and coordinate_status = NOT_APPLICABLE.
4. Create `source_reconciliation/p_pdf_coordinates.json` as an auxiliary,
   machine-readable ONE-ROW-PER-PDF-OBSERVATION coordinate index, including:
   article_id; observation_id; canonical occurrence_id (P ID for accepted
   observations, null for unresolved/excluded); source_id; PDF filename and
   hash; page_index; page_number; printed_page_label if actually shown;
   coordinate_system; page_width_pt and page_height_pt; page_rotation_degrees;
   raw_p_expression; bbox_pt (or null); coordinate_status; extraction_method;
   matching_text_or_words; and a short status_reason if not MEASURED. If one
   canonical P is independently represented by both main HTML and main PDF,
   preserve its HTML locator in the observations registry AND its PDF bbox in
   this PDF-only index, linked by observation_id/canonical P. For a given P,
   separate abstract, narrative, table and figure printings must retain separate
   P IDs and, where measurable, separate positions. Do not duplicate P merely
   to store an alternate representation of the same printed location.
5. Geometry MUST be traceable to the source. Validate the bbox lies within the
   recorded page rectangle, the source file/page exists, and the source literal
   can be recovered at or near the stored native PDF token location. Flag any
   mismatch, ambiguous match, duplicate token collision, coordinate transform
   uncertainty, or page-boundary clipping for review. A missing bbox MUST stay
   null with a specific reason (scanned-image-only source, figure image,
   inaccessible text geometry, ambiguous fragmented token, or unavailable
   extraction tool); a page-only locator is not a coordinate measurement.
6. Write `reports/p_coordinate_coverage.json` reporting, BY PDF and page:
   detected PDF source observations, MEASURED bbox count, UNAVAILABLE bbox
   count, NOT_RUN bbox count, and out-of-bounds/text-mismatch exceptions.
   Reconcile this report's PDF observation IDs with the candidate register and
   all accepted canonical P IDs that have a PDF representation. Every accepted
   PDF-observed P must have a linked coordinate record, even if null with a
   reason. Coordinate validation is a SEPARATE state from source completeness,
   scientific reconciliation, schema integrity and human verification: do not
   claim coordinate-complete when any applicable bbox remains unavailable or
   untested. Do not declare that these checks were executed unless actually run.
7. Keep this index in `source_reconciliation/` and its audit in `reports/`, NOT
   in `data/` as extra columns of the 25-table schema and NOT in any of the six
   human QC scripts. Preserve the existing exact schema and 302-field appendix.
   Human-facing R scripts retain a short page/table locator and P IDs; later
   provenance tools can resolve a P ID to its native PDF coordinates. If tool
   or time limits prevent coordinate collection, deliver the source-grounded
   extraction as PARTIAL for coordinate provenance, with a truthful NOT_RUN or
   UNAVAILABLE account, not a cosmetic/mock coordinate file.

SOURCE-TOKEN COVERAGE NOTE — EXACT P VALUES EQUAL TO 1.00
During the independent PDF p-column scan explicitly detect p expressions equal
to `1.00` (including a leading operator such as `P = 1.00`). These are valid
reported p-values when printed as statistical test results; do not discard
them as impossible or as probability thresholds. Distinguish them from effect
estimates of 1.00 using the source table column headings and coordinates.

MANDATORY DETERMINISTIC MACHINE VALIDATION — EXECUTE THE SUPPLIED TOOL
The existing Shiny handoff includes `VALIDATE_P_TO_R.py` with this START_HERE.
It is executable source code, not an article or a scientific reference. After
finishing P-to-R decisions and generating Module 01, save the output package to
a working directory and RUN this validator using Python 3. Example commands:

  python3 VALIDATE_P_TO_R.py /path/to/article_output \
    --module01 /path/to/article_output/qc/model_unverified/<article_id>_01_results_qc.R \
    --supplement-pdf /path/to/source/supplement.pdf \
    --output /path/to/article_output/reports/p_to_r_machine_validation.json

Include each actual supplemental PDF with a separate `--supplement-pdf` argument
when supported; do not invent PDF paths. The validator uses PyMuPDF when available
to check exact printed `1.00` p-value tokens against measured table-column
positions, because these tokens can be missed by a text-extraction pass. More
complete source coverage still requires the independent full-page scans described
above. A p=1.00 sentinel PASS does NOT certify that all other p-values were found.

The validator is a MANDATORY additional gate, not a substitute for independent
scientific judgment. It checks P conservation, per-P decision/map equivalence,
R anchor identities, accepted/rejected candidate-pair compatibility, R-module
P sets and secondary printings, source observations and targeted PDF coverage.
If it reports FAIL, inspect and correct the PROPOSED data and rerun it until
PASS, OR return a transparent PARTIAL package with the failures and unresolved
ledger intact. Never alter a frozen P inventory to make validation pass; source
corrections require a new recorded P-inventory version and reconciling downstream.
If native Python execution, source file access, or required PDF support is
unavailable, record NOT_RUN rather than claiming a structural PASS. Include the
validator report and exact command/status in `reports/`. Scientific identity
remains model-proposed until human source review. Do not set 'complete' simply
because a Python validator passed. The six-module R layout and the schema 3.1
scientific tables remain unchanged.

MANDATORY STATISTICAL-IDENTITY RECONCILIATION GATE — AFTER FREEZING P, BEFORE WRITING R/QC
This is a distinct scientific reasoning stage, NOT a continuation of PDF p-token
scanning. The source-coverage gate and coordinate inventory come FIRST. Reconciliation
MUST NOT alter/delete canonical printed P IDs, raw literals, observation-level PDF
coordinates, source positions, or the frozen per-source counts. If source coverage is
PARTIAL, reconcile only known P records and state that the inferred R total is PARTIAL.

A. FREEZE INPUT AND CONSTRUCT COMPARISON CONTEXT.
   Snapshot the finalized canonical P inventory and its row count in the run's
   machine audit; record a machine-checkable input fingerprint if available. Work
   from those frozen P records and source blocks rather than re-extracting printed
   tokens while assigning R. For each P gather the actual outcome definition and
   time origin, endpoint/timepoint, contrast and reference group, analysis unit and
   population/cohort, test or estimator, model family/stage (univariable versus
   multivariable), adjustment/matching/stratification, source display/row/footnotes,
   and effect/CI when printed. Explicitly represent unknown dimensions; do not
   fill missing scientific context from similar nearby rows.

B. PASS 1 — WITHIN-DISPLAY AND WITHIN-ANALYSIS CANDIDATE SEARCH.
   Partition P records by table and by defensible analysis family, keeping page-
   break continuation blocks together. Identify possible repeated reports and
   possible distinct tests within each family; review statistical Methods, captions,
   column headers and footnotes. In particular, determine whether multiple
   survival landmarks reproduce ONE global log-rank test or are independently
   tested; do not infer either answer from equal p literals. Consider whether
   alternate summaries (for example mean versus median of the same measure) show
   one comparison or distinct tests. Preserve omnibus categorical tests separately
   from category-level coefficient tests unless the source supports equivalence.
   Cox coefficients from different outcomes, contrasts, covariate levels, strata,
   model stages, adjusted/matched populations or time origins are NOT duplicates
   merely because the variable name and printed p-value match.

C. PASS 2 — CROSS-DISPLAY AND CROSS-NARRATIVE CANDIDATE SEARCH.
   For EVERY frozen P, check plausible equivalents in the same analysis family
   and in main tables, supplemental tables, figures/captions, Abstract and Results
   narrative. Do not skip narrative or figures because their R rows will be
   displayed elsewhere in Module 01. Generate plausible candidate pairs based on
   scientific context and labeled source links, NOT only numerical p equality.
   Search both directions: use source table results to locate narrative/figure
   reports AND use narrative/figure statements to locate underlying table tests.
   Keep the candidate comparison bounded by scientific analysis groups; do not
   attempt or claim an exhaustive all-versus-all comparison of unrelated tests.
   When no credible candidate exists after those searches, explicitly record
   `no_plausible_repeat_found` with the inspected source scope—not a fabricated
   rejected pair. Every P requires a documented candidate-search disposition.

D. ADJUDICATE STATISTICAL IDENTITY, NOT JUST STRING SIMILARITY.
   Evaluate each plausible P-to-P or P-to-existing-R candidate against outcome,
   time origin, population/risk set, predictor and reference comparison, test,
   model specification/stage, adjustment, matching and estimate/CI compatibility.
   Assign `same_test` ONLY with source-supported equivalence; assign
   `distinct_test` when materially different estimands/tests are established, or
   as a provisional singleton after a documented search found no plausible
   counterpart. If evidence is insufficient or conflicting, assign
   `unresolved_identity` (null R ID when a defensible assignment cannot be made),
   with explicit competing candidates and outstanding evidence. Identical p values
   are NEVER sufficient evidence; differing p literals do not by themselves prove
   different tests. If the same test is supported despite unequal printed values,
   link the P IDs to one R while separately recording a `reporting_discrepancy`
   containing BOTH unmodified raw literals and locators. Do not silently correct
   the publication or discard a P when text and table disagree.

E. WRITE A REVIEWABLE DECISION LEDGER BEFORE EMITTING RESULTS.
   Write `source_reconciliation/p_to_r_decisions.json` as an auxiliary audit,
   with EXACTLY one decision record for EVERY canonical P ID: `p_id`, frozen
   `source_block_id`/source locator, raw p expression, searched scopes, candidate
   R/P IDs examined (or explicit none), identity dimensions used, disposition
   (`same_test`, `distinct_test`, `unresolved_identity`), assigned `r_id` or null,
   evidence locator(s)/footnotes and concise decision rationale, plus any open
   issue/discrepancy IDs. Every repeated-report link needs positive source evidence;
   every rejected plausible link needs a concrete differentiating dimension.
   Additionally write `source_reconciliation/p_to_r_candidate_pairs.json` for
   evaluated plausible pairs with accepted/rejected/uncertain decision and the
   supporting/contradicting dimensions. Record only real evaluated comparisons;
   do not invent pair assessments. Keep reporting discrepancies in a separate
   independent ledger so a discrepancy is not confused with uncertainty about
   whether two printings represent the same test. These are AUDIT artifacts,
   not extra tables or editable human scripts in the frozen 25-table schema.

F. BUILD DISTINCT R ONLY FROM THOSE DECISIONS.
   Group P IDs into one R only after establishing statistical identity. Keep
   canonical R IDs stable if an existing assignment can be retained without
   collision; maintain a machine-readable alias/change ledger if a regrouping
   requires different R IDs. Never reassign P IDs, discard coordinates, or
   promote an unresolved equivalence to a new confidently distinct R to make
   the row counts match. Populate the existing `data/statistical_results.json`
   and `data/printed_occurrences.json` from the adjudicated mapping; provide a
   complete P-to-R map with explicitly null/unresolved assignments. Generate
   the unchanged six R review modules (especially Module 01's one R-centered,
   display-first tribble row with all linked P IDs and other_printings/locations)
   FROM this one canonical mapping, not via a separate independent re-extraction.

G. MACHINE-CHECK CONSERVATION AND REPORT RECONCILIATION COVERAGE.
   Verify: (1) canonical P IDs remain identical before and after reconciliation;
   (2) exactly one decision record exists per P and each P is either mapped to
   one R or explicitly unresolved; (3) the count of `same_test` + `distinct_test`
   + `unresolved_identity` P dispositions equals the frozen P count; (4) every
   accepted multi-P R has documented identity evidence; (5) each non-null R is
   represented once in Module 01 and its full P set matches `p_ids`, named
   `other_printings` and named `other_locations`; (6) unresolved P remain
   visible in Module 01 and the unresolved ledger; (7) all original source/PDF
   coordinates survive unchanged. Report in `reports/p_to_r_reconciliation.json`:
   frozen P total, candidate-search-coverage total, candidate pairs examined,
   accepted/rejected/unresolved pairs, singleton R count, multi-P R count and
   their P coverage, unresolved-identity P count, reporting-discrepancy count,
   proposed R total and whether the reconciliation gate PASSED, PARTIAL or NOT_RUN.
   Do not require a minimum number of merged R records: zero repeats can be a
   genuine finding, but all-one-to-one mapping without actual candidate search
   evidence does NOT establish completed reconciliation. Set certified final
   `n_unique_results` unknown/null when source coverage or identity reconciliation
   is incomplete; retain `n_unique_results_proposed` as provisional if useful.
   Native machine checks may be PASS only if executed on the saved outputs;
   scientific identity and human verification remain separate states.

STAGE C — METHODS, SCIENTIFIC QUESTIONS, AND CLAIMS
8. Capture article characteristics, true unit of observation/randomization/analysis, cohort,
   missing data, registration timing (separate from registration existence), power, primary
   endpoint vs one specific primary test, 95% CIs, multiplicity families, sensitivity/subgroups,
