MODULE 01 — HARD FORMAT CONTRACT (FOLLOW VERBATIM)
The R script begins with a short legend and then ONLY compact `tribble` review units,
ordered by the published DATA DISPLAYS: Main Table 1, Main Table 2, Figure 1, etc.
according to first appearance in the PDF; then Supplemental Tables/Figures in their
published order; finally narrative-only results that are not anchored to a display.
Do NOT start the review with Abstract and Results p-value blocks, and do NOT put
`data_printed_occurrences` or one 20-line `list()` per P below the quick-scan rows.
Full printed-occurrence/source-metadata records live in `data/*.json` and
`source_reconciliation/`, not in the human script. A display with no NEW distinct
R results still needs its printed P locations mapped in the machine archives;
those repeat appearances should be attached to the owning R record, not counted
as new R IDs.

Exactly ONE visible canonical row per distinct reconciled R across module 01:
- Anchor it at the first SUBSTANTIVE table/figure displaying that test, prioritizing
  a fully labeled source table over an abstract/partial figure; deterministic ties
  resolved by PDF display order. A result found only in narrative goes in the
  narrative-only section.
- Additional reports in abstract, results, tables or figures are P occurrences
  attached to the owning R row, not second canonical R rows or new tests.
- Every P ID maps to one R ID or explicit unresolved state; do NOT merge based only
  on equal numeric p-values, similar label or printed location. Test identity
  considers outcome operationalization, contrast, population, model stage, method,
  adjustment and time origin. Unresolved identity stays flagged and prevents a
  verified total-R count until adjudicated; do not make up R IDs to force closure.
  For a printed P that cannot yet be assigned to an R, include a visible QC row
  at its own display with `r_id = NA_character_`, its P ID and literal; flag it
  unresolved in the ledger instead of hiding it from the human review.
- One P is one logical printed location in the publication. HTML and PDF depictions
  of the SAME location are source observations, NOT new P occurrences. Assign P IDs
  after cross-format alignment in canonical publication order (PDF display order,
  then other narrative/source-only locations); retain alias maps if historical IDs
  predate this policy. Do not change P/R IDs merely by moving a row between displays.

MANDATORY `tribble` columns, in this exact order and exact spelling:
  r_id, p_value, p_ids, result_label, other_printings, other_locations
`r_id` is a scalar stable result/test ID. `p_value` is the exact primary displayed
literal including its operator. `p_ids` is `list(c("P...","P..."))`, primary P ID
first, followed by all repeated P IDs. `result_label` is a short source-faithful
human-readable contrast (not a hypothesis about causal validity).
`other_printings` is `list(c(P00002="<0.05", P00003="0.043"))` (named vector),
with the literal for EACH P ID after the first; use `list(character(0))` if none.
`other_locations` is a named character vector with the SAME non-primary P IDs,
for example `list(c(P00013="Abstract p 1"))`; use `list(character(0))` if none.
The exact location of EACH primary P is made visible by a concise, provenance-
derived R-script comment immediately preceding that R row (see requirement below);
the underlying frozen machine P row retains its structured physical locator. Repeat values
AND repeated locations are data cells, not comments. A reviewer can edit these
without editing the backend metadata. Because module 01 has ONE editable record for
an R+its P attachments, all corrected literals, linked P IDs and R assignments
must be exported from these named cells; do NOT maintain a second editable copy
of any of these values elsewhere in another R script or machine mirror.

