OPEN INFERENCE 3.1 — STAGE 2 OF 2: SCIENTIFIC CONTEXT, QUESTIONS, CLAIMS & INFERENTIAL FOOTPRINT
R-first interpretation contract · 2026-10-17

INPUTS
Read the attached OIP_SOURCE_MANIFEST.json for exact article/source identity. Attached: the ORIGINAL article (PDF and HTML) and complete supplements, the OIP31_RUNTIME.zip, plus THREE reviewed R files from Stage 1: 01_article_study_design_qc.R, 02_inferential_methods_qc.R, 03_results_p_to_r_qc.R (filenames may carry an article prefix). They were selected from the local Verified folder. Treat ONLY explicitly reviewed scopes as human-approved. A file's location is a workflow declaration, not proof the R was sourced or every row reviewed. If any of the three files is missing, belongs to another paper, or contains contradictory identity evidence, STOP/flag it. DO NOT recreate the frozen P inventory or rewrite R identifiers. Propose corrections in a separate exceptions R table rather than mutating Modules 01–03.

YOUR SOLE TASK IS TO ADD SCIENTIFIC MEANING
Read the R objects and explanatory comments directly; no JSON-to-R translation is required. Consult original PDFs, HTML, Methods, legends and complete hierarchical tables whenever needed. Match by inherited row heading + column/outcome heading + source position, not by an isolated label such as 'Yes vs No'. In one bounded source table / model-outcome family at a time, identify population, outcome, predictor, contrast, reference, model type, adjusted covariates (only if supported), effect/CI and test unit for every R. Do not confuse adjustment for confounding with multiplicity correction. Missing context is unresolved, not fabricated. Stable R IDs are immutable references to the Stage-1 reviewed result identities.

GENERATE THREE EDITABLE R QC MODULES IN THIS ORDER
04_models_outcomes_context_qc.R — model family G, model-specific predictors, outcomes, population, contrast/reference, effect/CI, adjustment covariates, author-DAG evidence (only if present) versus model-proposed DAG hypotheses if requested; contextual evidence and source positions for each R, no unsupported filled-in labels. Reconstruct the context BEFORE wording operational questions.
05_questions_scientific_links_qc.R — author-stated scientific question registry Q, operational question per interpretable R (based on reviewed Stage-1 data + reconstructed Stage-2 context), and R→Q associations. Author-stated, author-reported analysis, and model-inferred questions MUST be separate types. Multi-R per Q and multiple Q per R are allowed, and some R may have no defensible Q linkage. Avoid templated non-questions ('is outcome associated with Yes vs No?'). Track parent predictor, outcome, reference and uncertainty. Quality gate must flag every generic/insufficient operational question.
06_claims_inference_footprint_qc.R — author major claims C from original text with exact quote/locator, proposed R→C evidence links without treating significance as causal or supportive by default, plus scientific alignment, stated-vs-operationalized footprint, multiplicity by justified outcome/question/model families, reported-vs-unreported corrections, planned vs post-hoc evidence, descriptive vs confirmatory claims. Missing prespecification evidence is NOT proof of post hoc testing. Repeated printed P for one R is not additional tests. Consider Bayesian outputs where present; do not use frequentist multiplicity arithmetic blindly.

QUALITY AND EXECUTION
Source-driven, no mass auto-generated generic phrases. A single reporting block can reuse source-level context but every R's outcome/predictor/contrast has to be explicit or unresolved. Validate R IDs against 03, question IDs against Q registry, claim IDs against C registry; produce machine-readable exception/conflict summary and provenance. If scale is too large, process source tables incrementally with saved bounded checkpoints and honestly report incomplete scopes. Never fabricate blanket verification or mark human QC complete. Reuse exact 3.1 schema definitions from companion runtime for final JSON/scientific assembly where feasible, but do not let schema completeness requirements degrade meaningful R QC content. Preserve all original Stage-1 R verbatim in the output archive; generate only 04–06, with proposed corrections separately.


MANDATORY HUMAN-QC READABILITY AND BOUNDED-SCOPE CONTRACT (PRESENTATION ONLY)
This contract applies to every R module produced in this stage. It changes rendering and review scope size ONLY; it must not change the OIP schema, R object contents, P/R identities, evidence, sources, or semantic classifications to achieve attractive formatting.

A. BOUNDED, INDEPENDENT QC SCOPES
- Divide each module into small scientifically coherent R objects, each immediately followed by its OWN commented `# qc_confirm("SCOPE_ID")`. Aim for 5–10 records/fields per metadata or methods scope, and 10–25 distinct R result rows per high-volume scope; use scientific boundaries over arbitrary row counts. Never impose a hard row limit that breaks one statistical identity or one model family.
- Suggested Module 01 scopes: article identification/authorship; design and setting; eligibility/population and sampling; endpoints/definitions; registration, protocol and prespecification. Suggested Module 02 scopes: inferential framework and nominal tests; survival/model methods; selection/matching; power/thresholds; multiplicity and Bayesian details; model/DAG provenance. Preserve any existing object/column contract required by the companion runtime; if one original table spans several QC scopes, use uniquely named compatible objects and document how they concatenate without ambiguity.
- Module 03: one publication table or scientifically meaningful row group per scope, generally 10–25 R rows. Split a large table with sequential suffixes (_01, _02...), but repeat the exact original table title, physical source locator, column/outcome hierarchy and relevant population above EVERY group. Show all P IDs for each R in the same record; do not split a multi-P statistical identity between groups. Cross-table identities remain linked via canonical R IDs with explicit notes.
- Modules 04–06: break by source table/model-outcome family, Q group, claim, or inference theme; keep the context needed to interpret each block. Large tables must be chunked into source-coherent named scopes rather than a single enormous tibble.
- A `qc_confirm` call pertains to one nearby complete object/scope ONLY. Never pre-uncomment it or set a human review status based on code generation, file placement, or machine PASS. Avoid reusing one scope ID across different blocks.

B. READABLE R CODE LAYOUT
- For short metadata/methods rows: prefer one field/value per physical R source line. Begin with a comment bearing a concise human heading, then place every cell on its own line, separated by blank lines between records. Put a short inline `# column_name` comment after an R value only if it remains syntactically valid and does not conceal data. Put schema column headers visibly above the block.
- Example valid layout (illustrative only):
    qc_article_identity <- tibble::tribble(
      ~field_id, ~value, ~source_locator, ~source_quote_or_inspection, ~evidence_classification, ~review_status,
      # FIRST AUTHOR
      "first_author",              # field_id
      "Andrew T. Li",             # value
      "Main PDF p1",             # source_locator
      "Andrew T. Li, MBBS",      # source_quote_or_inspection
      "reported",                # evidence_classification
      "model_proposed",          # review_status
      # JOURNAL / CITATION
      "journal_citation", "J Am Acad Dermatol. 2025;92(1):58–67",
      "Main PDF p1", "J Am Acad Dermatol 2025;92:58–67.",
      "reported", "model_proposed"
    )
    # qc_confirm("ARTICLE_IDENTITY")
  For production, maximize consistency: ideally all long records use the same one-cell-per-line pattern, with accurate explicit column comments to aid navigation. The example illustrates layout, not a requirement to create a new schema.
- Do NOT pad all text columns based on the longest quotation: this creates extreme horizontal scrolling. Alignment is optional only for naturally short fields; readable vertical cells are primary.
- For high-volume results, use RStudio `# ORIGINAL TABLE TITLE — GROUP 01 ----` section headings, blank lines between result records, and a short header with population, source page and column hierarchy. Keep long `result_label`, effect/CI, source excerpt and coordinates on their own physical lines. Put `r_id` and `p_ids` at the start of each record in the same consistent order. Do not create a single giant 300-character source line if avoidable.
- Do not truncate, escape incorrectly, summarize away, or silently alter exact source quotes, p-value literals, coordinates, effect estimates, list-valued cells, NA types, IDs, R symbols, or `qc_confirm` semantics. Source-faithfulness outranks formatting. Use syntactically valid R strings and `list(c(...))` where required.

C. MACHINE AND REVIEW SAFEGUARDS
- Each `.R` must source/parse normally; preserve named columns, their order, types and list-column semantics. Do not change existing schema tables or the six-module scientific division. Distinguish `model_proposed`, human-reviewed scopes, and unresolved values faithfully.
- When possible, run R parse/source checks and compare normalized evaluated objects to a pre-format equivalent. If R is unavailable, explicitly report R NOT RUN; perform static balanced-structure and row/column checks with available tools, and never claim semantic identity verified solely from visual format.
- README shall include a short navigation index: module → QC scope ID → source-table / topic → R object. The reviewer should be able to copy ONE complete scope (comments + data + confirmation) to a new ChatGPT review conversation.
- Preserve the existing article files, source-freeze evidence, complete P→R map, module filenames, and two-stage handoff contract. Do not add extra required R modules or require manual review for machine progression.


DELIVERY
Produce a downloadable Stage-2 ZIP preserving the same QC structure. Put the three NEW immutable-original model-proposed R modules 04–06 under qc/model_unverified/ AND byte-identical editable WORKING COPIES directly under qc/human_verified/ (not Verified/); retain the supplied Stage-1 R modules 01–03 under qc/human_verified/Verified/ byte-for-byte (and document their hashes). Keep qc/human_verified/ available for in-progress manual review and qc/human_verified/Verified/ as a separate subfolder for explicitly reviewed R scripts; add README files so empty folders survive ZIP extraction. Modules 04–06 in qc/human_verified/ start model_proposed, with all qc_confirm calls commented, and are NOT approved just because they are in this folder. Do not re-label any Module 04–06 as reviewed. Include a concise inferential footprint summary, unresolved items, tests actually run, and any supported schema 3.1 scientific data. NEVER report full-schema PASS if required final tables were omitted. The R scripts are the primary human/model handoff; machine JSON, if generated, is derived and cannot overrule reviewed R. No automatic posting or upload is implied.


MANDATORY QC FOLDER WORKING-COPY ASSERTIONS
- qc/model_unverified/ contains the untouched, originally model-proposed R modules for the current stage.
- qc/human_verified/ contains direct-child editable copies of the current stage R modules with identical content at creation. User edits these copies in place. Never modify their scientific content during copying.
- qc/human_verified/Verified/ exists separately and begins empty for current-stage modules. Only explicit human-reviewed scopes can be represented as verified; folder position is not evidence of approval. Existing Stage-1 inputs (when present) are preserved byte-for-byte and are not overwritten.
- Validate that each expected current-stage filename exists in BOTH model_unverified/ and human_verified/, and that their initial SHA-256 digests match. If any check fails, label package PARTIAL and explain the defect. Do not report a complete handoff.
- Include README files explaining working-copy editing, qc_confirm scope approval, and that model_unverified/ is untouched for comparison.

MANDATORY COVERAGE SEMANTICS
- Report separately: (a) number of R identities accounted for in an inventory, (b) number with an individualized reconstructed source-context record, (c) number with a source-grounded operational question, (d) number with R→Q mapping, and (e) number pending contextualization.
- Never describe 100% ID accounting as 100% scientific contextualization. On partial runs, retain all pending R IDs in a coverage index and mark scientific enrichment incomplete. Do not fabricate generic questions to force completion.
- Large R tables (including the coverage index) must be broken into bounded, coherent QC scopes with their own qc_confirm calls; a single 1,000-row table with one approval call is not acceptable for human review. Keep schema-compatible column names and preserve exact ID coverage.

