<div class="oi-callout-body">

### Multiplicity: multiple named, source-linked practices {.unlisted}

Multiplicity is **reporting-based and scope-aware**, not one mutually exclusive adjusted/unadjusted article label. An article may report Holm for co-primary outcomes, Benjamini–Hochberg for a different comparison set, explicitly unadjusted exploratory analyses and a multiplicity limitation. Preserve **multiple repeatable records** with a controlled method name/error-control family (FWER, FDR, other), source-faithful wording, evidence locator, and scope. **`scope_unclear` is explicitly permitted** when the paper does not identify the relevant family or tests. An article-level Paper 1 summary is **derived**; overlapping methods or statements need not be forced into one category.

Distinguish an explicit no-adjustment statement, no mention of adjustment, method named without scope, acknowledgement of risk and truly unresolved/contradictory reporting. **Covariate adjustment is not multiplicity control.** R validators should enforce vocabularies and required evidence; invalid values should fail rather than be silently recoded. These repeatable multiplicity structures are proposed/implemented in the lean review candidate, **not an investigator-approved new canonical schema**.

### Registration, protocols and prespecification {.unlisted}

**Stage I's evidence corpus is only the published article and supplied supplements**, including supplied protocols and analysis plans. It records reported registration identifiers and dates but **does not perform routine external registry lookups or historical amendment audits**. Registration mentioned, registration prospectively supported, SAP reported and outcomes or plans supported as prospectively prespecified are separate measurements. Unknown timing stays unknown. Nonreporting is not proof that registration or planning did not occur. An external verification layer is possible later and must retain separate provenance.

### Inferential methods and results {.unlisted}

The article-level framework can be frequentist, Bayesian, mixed or unresolved. A Bayesian study can legitimately print zero p-values while reporting scientific inference. Paper 1 may record a minimal broad methods signal and a simple indicator of reported covariate adjustment where justified; it does **not** require reconstruction of every regression, Cox model, DAG, test instance or causal-identification strategy.

**Printed P and distinct R counts are separate observables.** A complete physical P inventory can be reported even if P→R reconciliation is unresolved; a missing cited supplement makes an article-wide total unknown and observed counts a lower bound. Under dictionary draft.4, `result_summary.n_unique_results_proposed` is a **proposed hypothesis-test-R** count, not necessarily the count of all R. Report test-R and all-R separately with appropriate completeness qualifications; never equate R with independent hypotheses.

