The original OIP 3.1.0 schema and 3.1.1-draft.4 dictionary in the app/runtime are **legacy support reference only** for this Paper 1 pilot. They are NOT the active 99-field scientific schema and their validators cannot certify it. Field names/codes/definitions are exactly Appendix A; old aliases are only documented by the accompanying old-to-new crosswalk. Do not silently coerce a new field into an old canonical field. The editable R modules, once source-reviewed and signed, are the human-reviewed science; machine JSON is provisional provenance. There is no mandatory verified-R→JSON re-export. Do not infer any scientific values from historical example text.

Start extraction when this contract, a supplied main article and source packet are present and uploads are complete. If sources are pending, wait for them; if sources are absent, request them. On actual execution produce an article-specific output ZIP, not a narrative promise. Do not fake R-native, Shiny, source inspection or human QC test results. This document does not control the model's hidden thinking level or configuration.

## 01. SCIENTIFIC OWNERSHIP AND THREE REVIEW MODULES

Stage I encompasses actual source inspection and complete numeric PPO inventory, comprehensive PIO inventory (including P-free inference), all source-identifiable DIRs, the DPR subset, analysis-group context, classification, article/study metadata and reporting methods, registration, prespecification, multiplicity. A Stage I source-local inferential finding can be unlinked to any author claim. Stage II will reconstruct distinct outcomes, questions, explicit claims, model SPEC/FIT and links (potentially many-to-many), with new scoped human review. Never assume that a DIR is a model fit, a causal estimand, a question, a claim, or independent test.

Create exactly three reviewer modules named `<article_id>_01_publication_metadata.R`, `<article_id>_02_study_and_reporting_methods_qc.R`, `<article_id>_03_p_to_r_reconciliation_qc.R` (the third title is historical but holds all PPO/PIO/DIR). Script 01 is routine metadata; script 02 has independent vertical domain QC; script 03 has nine visible columns and source-organized bounded QC. Every script has a matching R-derived HTML view and a frozen proposal plus editable working copy. Keep initial `qc_confirm()` calls commented.

## 02. PREFLIGHT, SOURCE INVENTORY, AND PROVENANCE

1. Inspect the manifest first when supplied. Preserve its exact `article_id`, filenames, source IDs, roles, and hashes; verify those hashes against accessible bytes. Never silently add/remove an `S` prefix. Compare manifest identity with the actual title, DOI/PII, and citation. Conflicting article identities block combined extraction.
2. If no manifest exists, build a clearly labeled manifest from supplied files and a source-supported publication identifier. Record that the manifest was created in this run. If no reliable identifier is available, use a declared run-local provisional key and block canonical integration; never guess an identifier, volume, issue, or publication year from a filename fragment.
3. Record each file's format, original filename, byte size and SHA-256 when bytes are available, logical document role, and relation to other representations. Distinguish a main PDF and main HTML representing one publication from a separately authored supplement. DOCX/PDF renderings of the same supplement are alternate representations, not two scientific supplements. Record transformation provenance when a supplied PDF was rendered from DOCX.
4. Identify all numbered tables, figures, appendices, supplements, protocols, and SAPs referenced in the main article and source manifest. Mark each supplied, absent, unreadable, or not yet assessed. A linked but unsupplied protocol is `mentioned_only`, not `supplied`.
5. Create an inventory of inspectable source regions: abstract; main prose by section; each table with continuations; each figure/panel and caption; each supplemental table/figure; and relevant protocol/SAP sections. Include zero-result regions only after actual inspection. A blank inspection log is not proof of zero results.
6. Save exact accessible instruction/dictionary snapshots, their hashes, source manifest, run ID, actual timestamps, and actual tool/environment information. Record model identity or effort only when genuinely available. Do not reconstruct inaccessible system prompts or invent execution metadata.
7. If prior extraction packages or reviewed R files are supplied, inspect them before re-extracting. Reuse compatible frozen source inventories rather than silently starting over. Record the resumed run/version and the remaining work. Earlier human decisions remain immutable references; conflicts require a proposed revision, not automatic replacement.

Keep four questions separate: Were the supplied sources inspected? Do their representations agree? Are scientific result identities reconciled? Has a human reviewed the relevant fields? A machine check answers none of the other questions by itself.

## 03. SOURCE BLOCKS, TWO SOURCE PASSES, AND LABEL ANCHORS

### 03.1 Establish block context before assigning scientific labels

For every table or meaningful analysis block, record its exact printed title, heading hierarchy, physical boundaries, population/cohort, outcome or characteristic, model-column headings, parent predictors, reference categories, and applicable footnotes. Record only what the source supports; identify missing dimensions. For narrative or an untitled display, use a labeled locator, not an invented verbatim title.

Freeze a source-block registry before downstream identity reconciliation. At each new table, heading, model column, or parent predictor, reset context. At a page break, establish a continuation using source evidence; do not propagate the previous endpoint merely because no new heading was detected. Check the first, last, and transition-adjacent rows of every block. A row such as “Yes versus No” requires its actual parent predictor. A demographic table following a survival table must not inherit the survival endpoint.

Store grouping for source navigation, not as a new formal outcome, model, or multiplicity ontology. Shared context may be reused only when the printed layout/footnotes support it. A single common Methods paragraph does not automatically assign a particular test to every P.

### 03.2 Perform two separately documented source inspections

Pass A and Pass B are source-referenced observations, not two copies of one extraction. Use different reading/traversal strategies where practical. Save each pass before comparing them; do not fill the second pass to match the first pass's counts. These are procedural checks, not a claim of statistically independent models or blinded human readers.

- **HTML and PDF supplied:** Both passes inspect both representations and every supplied supplemental/figure source. Preserve source-specific observations. Compare A versus B within each source, then compare equivalent HTML/PDF locations.
- **PDF only:** Perform native text/geometry inspection where available and a separately documented layout/page inspection. Inspect tables, captions, figures, and continuation pages. HTML comparison is `NOT_APPLICABLE`, not failed or a fictional zero count.
- **HTML only:** Use semantic DOM/table traversal and a separate section/structured-text inspection. Inspect supplied image assets. Record actual DOM/section/table/cell locators. Do not invent PDF pages or coordinates.
- **DOCX or other supplied supplements:** Prefer source-native table structure and verify the relevant layout. Compare any supplied PDF representation independently. Inspect the actual figures/embedded images where textual extraction is insufficient.

Images and page layouts must be inspected when they contain statistical evidence not captured reliably by text. Use available file/page-image tools; do not claim image inspection from a text search alone. Use OCR only when necessary and record its limitations.

For each PDF-derived accepted observation retain measured page index and bounding box where tools permit, including coordinate units, origin, page size, and printed page label when available. Do not manufacture approximate coordinates and call them measured. Missing geometry is a separately reported provenance gap; it does not justify deleting a valid P. HTML-only observations have no PDF-coordinate requirement.

### 03.3 Compare observations and audit labels

Match alternate representations using logical source location, table/row hierarchy, column, heading, and comparison context. Equal numbers alone are insufficient. Report matched, source-only, value-discrepant, label-discrepant, and uncertain observations. A figure supplied both as an image and embedded in a PDF is not two publication locations.

Preserve disagreement evidence and both raw forms. A documented display-location match may establish one logical P even if format renderings disagree; the canonical representative is not a scientific correction. When location equivalence cannot be established, retain the source candidates as unresolved and withhold a complete canonical P total.

Audit labels against actual headings/footnotes in a separate check. Do not regenerate “source evidence” from assigned labels. Unanchored identity context may leave a P fully observed but its R assignment unresolved. Do not let unresolved labels erase source occurrences or imply that a complete physical P count is impossible when only R identity is uncertain.

## 04. PRINTED P OCCURRENCES: INCLUSION, EXCLUSION, AND FREEZE

### 04.1 Unit of observation

A canonical PID denotes one explicit numeric p-value expression at one logical publication reporting location. A table cell repeated as HTML/PDF shares a PID. The same study result printed in abstract, Results, a table, a figure caption, and a supplement has separate PIDs, even if every value is identical. A second extraction pass, repeated DOM rendering of the same location, or overlapping processing chunk does not create another PID.

**D1 — proposed numeric-P convention.** Historical materials sometimes included significance marks in P and sometimes separated numeric P from symbolic annotations. This draft uses numeric expressions for `p_occurrences`, while retaining symbol-only evidence in a separate annotation ledger. Do not silently apply this convention to historical inventories. Report the definition with every count.

Include explicit equality, inequality, range, and scientific-notation p expressions wherever they report this article's empirical analysis, including p-values of `1`, `1.00`, `0.000`, and values above significance thresholds. Bare numeric cells under a p-value header qualify. Preserve exactly what is printed: `.04` stays `.04`; `<.001` stays `<.001`; a bare cell does not acquire an invented `P =` prefix. Keep the column header as evidence. Do not turn an inequality into an exact value or repair a rounded zero.

Distinct explicit p-expressions in one sentence or cell each receive their own PID when separable. A single printed interval such as `.01 < P < .05` is one expression, not two. A numeric p-expression in a figure is P; inspect the actual mark and comparison, not only its caption.

Exclude alpha/significance conventions, power-design assumptions, screening cutoffs, illustrative calculation examples, and results merely quoted from other studies from the empirical P inventory. Store relevant methodological declarations as methods evidence. Classify by meaning, not location: a Methods section can contain a genuine empirical diagnostic result; a Results paragraph can repeat a significance convention.

Do not infer P from confidence intervals, posterior probabilities, a significance adjective, boldface, asterisks, or test statistics. `NS` and `*` are not numeric P expressions. Preserve any explicit symbol-to-threshold legend and its actual mark/comparison links in the annotation ledger; do not multiply a legend across unspecified panels or convert a mark into a fabricated numeric printing. Such evidence is a source-block hint for Stage II, not a Stage I PID or RID under Section 07.

A value labeled `q` is not automatically a p-value. Preserve the exact author terminology and classify the evidence explicitly; unresolved p-versus-q interpretation stays in the candidate/annotation ledger. Numeric multiplicity-adjusted values expressly reported as p-values are P; retain their adjustment context.

### 04.2 Shared and aggregate expressions

**D5 — proposed aggregate-expression rule.** A single printed statement “all P < .05” is one physical expression, not an invented P for every implied comparison. If it clearly denotes one omnibus test, it may map to that one R. If it covers multiple distinct tests, the one-P-to-one-R schema cannot encode a many-R assignment: keep the PID visible with `mapping_status = unresolved`, `rid = NA`, and a rationale naming the aggregate scope. Do not choose a representative RID or fabricate extra PIDs. Separately source-identifiable results can be recorded under Section 07; the aggregate PID remains unresolved until an explicit schema extension or adjudication resolves it.

### 04.3 Canonical table and alternate representations

`p_occurrences` contains exactly one row per `(article_id, pid)`. `source_id`, `source_locator`, and `ppo_raw_p_expression` identify one documented representative observation. All alternate source observations, pass labels, exact expressions, and geometry remain in the evidence ledger and link to that PID.

Choose a representative by recorded source-fidelity/readability criteria, not significance or preferred numerical value. Source-format discrepancies remain visible and flagged even when a representative is selected. No PDF/HTML summation, numerical uniqueness operation, or maximum-of-source-counts heuristic is an acceptable replacement for logical occurrence accounting.

`p_occurrences.ppo_result_label` uses source wording and enough source-derived row/header context to identify the printed comparison. Label any paraphrase; do not fabricate quotation. `results.dir_result_label` is the concise canonical description of the distinct result and need not be a verbatim sentence.

Assign stable PIDs in documented source order. Preserve IDs across resumed runs and approved corrections when possible. Gaps in IDs are allowed. Once candidate inclusion and representation reconciliation are complete, freeze the canonical P records, count, source mappings, raw literals, and hash. Source corrections later require a new recorded P-inventory version, a change ledger, and downstream revalidation; semantic reconciliation cannot modify the frozen observations.

If inspection is incomplete, freeze the observed portion with its explicit scope. Report observed numeric P as a lower bound only when accepted P identities are settled and the limitation is missing source coverage. Unresolved possible format duplicates require a count qualification rather than an unjustified lower-bound claim.

## 05. PAPER 1 ARTICLE-LEVEL SCIENTIFIC ABSTRACTION

Every main measurement requires a source locator and supporting quote, labeled paraphrase, or documented negative inspection. Record a concise source-to-code justification and caveat when necessary—not a private reasoning transcript. Read the relevant prose, tables, footnotes, figures, and supplied supplements together. Do not make absence claims from one unsuccessful keyword search.

Use the exact codes in Appendix A. `not_reported`, `explicit_none`, `unclear`, `undetermined`, `not_applicable`, and typed missing values are not interchangeable. Select a listed uncertainty code when it fits; use true missing for unperformed abstraction or missing free-text/integer values, with a reason in evidence. Do not add “mixed” to a field whose dictionary does not permit it. Contradictions across sources remain visible. Multiple study components must not be collapsed into a misleading single answer; use an available `mixed`/uncertainty code, scoped evidence, or a documented schema limitation.

### 05.1 Design and size

Classify intervention assignment, timing, and randomization separately. An observational study is not necessarily retrospective; a prospective study is not necessarily randomized. Record the actual unit of allocation in evidence without adding a new main measurement.

`randomized` requires source-supported random allocation. If an author label and the described allocation mechanism conflict, preserve both and use `unclear` until adjudicated rather than silently correcting the authors or accepting the title alone. `not_applicable` denotes no study-assigned allocation, not an unreported method in an interventional study.

`study_primary_analysis_n` is the number of units in the identified primary analysis, not automatically the screened, enrolled, randomized, or largest cohort. Record the analysis scope and counted unit. Do not convert lesions into participants, count repeated observations as independent people, sum overlapping populations, or select the largest available N. If no single primary-analysis denominator is supported, leave `study_primary_analysis_n` missing and explain. `study_primary_analysis_unit` can be known even when N is not. Multiple co-primary populations with different denominators require explicit uncertainty, not an arbitrary choice.

### 05.2 Registration and supplied documentation

Separate `registration_reporting_status`, `registration_registry_identifier`, and `registration_timing_status`. A recognizable study-registration ID supports `id_reported`; a registry used as a data source or an ethics approval number does not, by itself, establish registration of this study. Preserve the identifier verbatim without commentary in the identifier field.

Timing depends on supplied dated evidence. An ID, the word “prospective” describing study design, or a statement of registration alone does not establish registration before enrollment. Use `undetermined` when chronology is unsupported. `not_applicable` for registration timing applies to explicit no registration, not merely no reported ID. Do not query a live registry in this profile.

Assess protocol and SAP separately. A document included in the packet is `supplied`; a citation/link with no document is `mentioned_only`. Mere existence of Methods is not a separately supplied SAP. An undated protocol can be supplied without supporting prospective prespecification. Preserve conflicts between versions without performing a historical amendment audit.

### 05.3 Primary endpoint and prespecification

`prespec_primary_endpoint_declaration_status` asks whether authors explicitly designate a primary endpoint/outcome. Preserve its name, definition, and any co-primary wording in evidence. Do not infer primacy from abstract prominence, sample-size discussion alone, smallest P, or clinical importance.

`prespec_primary_endpoint_status` and `prespec_analysis_status` require their own timing evidence. A declared primary endpoint does not prove it was prospectively specified. An unsupported author claim of prespecification is retained but coded `undetermined`, not discarded and not promoted to dated support. Describe the relevant temporal anchor and the dated evidence supporting it. A plan date without evidence of its relation to enrollment/data availability/analysis does not by itself establish the required chronology. Where the appropriate temporal anchor cannot be established, remain `undetermined`.

`explicit_post_hoc` requires an explicit source statement for the relevant endpoint or analysis. Do not infer post hoc practice from absent registration, a retrospective design, or many analyses. `not_applicable` requires a substantive explanation that no relevant primary endpoint/plan is definable; absence of documentation alone is insufficient.

No unique “primary P” is required in this profile. Several distinct tests may address one declared outcome; preserve their R identities without selecting the significant one as primary.

### 05.4 Inferential reporting

Classify `reporting_inference_framework` from the analyses actually reported in the supplied corpus, not the journal, software name, or one isolated method word. Retain evidence for each framework when coding `mixed`. A Bayesian article can legitimately have P = 0. Posterior probabilities are not automatically p-values. Do not convert uncertainty about the framework into `none_reported`; that code requires complete relevant review without reported inference. The classification does not adjudicate the analysis's scientific validity.

`reporting_alpha_status` asks whether a significance threshold is explicitly stated. `reporting_alpha_verbatim` preserves the exact threshold, operator, sidedness, and scope where supplied. Preserve multiple scoped thresholds rather than forcing one global alpha. Do not infer alpha = .05 from conventional usage or from which findings the authors call significant. Legend definitions can be threshold evidence without being empirical P occurrences.

`reporting_covariate_adjustment_status` identifies reported analysis adjusting for covariates. Distinguish it from multiplicity correction, a paired design, and general matching/stratification statements whose adjustment role is not established. Preserve what “adjusted” actually refers to. An explicit no-covariate-adjustment statement is retained in evidence; do not invent an `explicit_none` code for this field. When the full relevant record supports no reported adjusted analysis, use its existing `not_reported` code and retain that stronger source statement.

## 06. MULTIPLICITY: REPEATABLE, SCOPED STATEMENTS

Read statistical Methods, endpoint hierarchy, table/figure footnotes, Results qualifications, Discussion limitations, and all supplied methodological supplements. `reporting_multiplicity_status` is a reporting indicator, not a judgment that control was adequate or applied to every result.

Record each distinct source-supported method/scope or nonadjustment/limitation statement in `multiplicity_statements`. Multiple procedures and apparently different strategies may coexist without conflict when their scopes differ. Repeated appearances of the same statement can share one statement record, with all source evidence retained. One sentence naming two methods produces two method records; one method used for two explicitly different scopes may need two records.

For each record preserve `mult_statement_type`, one exact permitted `mult_adjustment_method`, `mult_adjustment_method_verbatim`, `mult_scope_status`, `mult_scope_as_reported`, `mult_error_criterion_as_reported`, source ID, locator, and verbatim evidence. Do not manufacture a statement record to encode silence.

- Use `adjustment_method` when adjustment is reported. A named unlisted method uses `other_named`; unnamed adjustment uses `adjustment_unspecified`. Do not map an acronym to a named method without sufficient source evidence.
- Use `explicit_no_adjustment` only for an actual nonadjustment statement for its stated scope. “Nominal” wording is evidence to preserve, not an automatic whole-article no-adjustment claim.
- Use `acknowledgment_only` for a multiplicity-risk/limitation statement. This does not preclude adjustment-method records elsewhere.
- Use `conflicting_statement` for genuinely unresolved contradictory reporting about the same scope; retain both sides and cross-reference them in evidence. Do not call scoped differences contradictions.

Unknown scope is `scope_status = unclear`, not an invented formal hypothesis family. `not_applicable` is not a substitute for missing scope on an adjustment statement.

The error criterion is **as reported**. Code FWER or FDR only when the authors explicitly name that criterion; a familiar procedure name alone is not permission to populate it. If the statement does not name a criterion, use `not_stated`; use `not_applicable` only when the concept truly does not apply to that statement. Method knowledge must not masquerade as source text.

For a statement that is not an adjustment procedure, `method_code = not_applicable` is normally appropriate. Retain the exact nonadjustment or limitation wording in `source_evidence`.

A supported positive statement can establish `multiplicity_reporting = reported` even when other coverage is incomplete, provided that limitation is documented. `not_reported` requires complete relevant inspection. Unresolved conflicting/incomplete evidence that prevents a classification uses `unclear`. Never derive “no multiplicity adjustment” merely from missing reporting, or label all P as unadjusted because the article lacks a multiplicity statement.

## 07. COMPREHENSIVE INFERENTIAL INVENTORY: PPO, PIO, DPR AND DIR

### 07.1 Four units and their keys

PPO (Printed P-value Occurrence) is one *numeric p-value expression* at one logical reporting location, identified by `ppo_id` (display PID). PIO (Printed Inferential Occurrence) is one source-local reporting instance of an identifiable inferential target, identified by `pio_id`. DIR (Distinct Inferential Result) is one source-identifiable inference about an estimand, contrast, parameter or hypothesis within an analysis context, identified by `dir_id` (display RID). DPR (Distinct P-associated Result) is the subset of DIRs with at least one positively mapped PPO; DPR does NOT get its own ID namespace or an independent row table.

Do not require PIO to numerically exceed PPO: one PIO may contain two nominal/adjusted p expressions, each with its own PPO. A point estimate, its compatible confidence interval and its p-value are **one PIO reporting one DIR** where they describe the same target. The same target printed in abstract and table yields two PIOs and may have two PPOs, but one DIR. Alternate HTML and PDF renderings of one logical location share occurrence identifiers. Distinct inferential targets sharing one physical table row generate distinct PIOs, not one row-wide PIO.

`dir_id`, `pio_id` and `ppo_id` are three disjoint ID namespaces, unique within an article. All four scientific types are explicit in the field definitions and reviewer interface. Never deduplicate by matching printed numbers or matching text labels. Stable IDs must remain stable across QC; proposed identity merges/splits require recorded versioned events.

### 07.2 PIO eligibility and reporting form

Include a p-bearing inferential test; a source-identifiable hypothesis test reported only as statistic or symbolic annotation; an estimate with inferential uncertainty such as a confidence interval, standard error or graphical inferential interval; an explicitly model-based inferential estimate; or posterior probability/credible interval/Bayes-factor inference with identifiable target. Preserve exact reported statements, estimators, confidence vs credible terminology, source titles/headings, legend and footnotes. Distinct arm-specific survival estimates at separately reported landmarks are separate PIOs and usually separate DIRs when their inferential target differs, even if produced by one KM estimate procedure. Do not count an arbitrary plotted curve point, raw data, descriptive SD/IQR, individual MCMC draw or a bare number without supported inferential character. Mark questionable borderline evidence as a candidate with source and rationale; do not count as accepted PIO until eligibility is supported.

`pio_reporting_form` is exactly `test_only`, `estimate_only`, `estimate_and_test`, `posterior_summary`, or `other_or_unclear`. This describes *what the source prints*, distinct from the DIR-level inferential classification. Include a qualifying target with unresolved distinct identity as an accepted PIO with `pio_dir_mapping_status=unresolved` and missing `pio_dir_dir_id` when its inferential eligibility is clear. Do not erase accepted PIO merely because mapping to an RID is uncertain. Candidate whose target/eligibility cannot be determined is NOT an accepted PIO and stays in the candidate ledger.

### 07.3 DIR identity, granularity and classification

DIR means a distinct source-identifiable *inferential finding*, not a question, outcome, hypothesis family, independent test, fitted model or unique numeric result. Compare target/estimand, population/cohort and unit, contrast and reference, timepoint/time origin, estimator/test, adjusted vs unadjusted analysis, model column/variant, effect parameter, and reporting provenance. Several coefficients in one fitted Cox model ordinarily generate several DIRs. Inserting age as an adjustment covariate without reporting an age inferential coefficient does **not** generate a new DIR; reporting the age HR with CI or p-value may. A global log-rank comparison is one test DIR; separately reported arm-by-landmark KM confidence-interval estimates are additional *estimation* DIRs, not additional tests. An HR + compatible CI + P count as one DIR, not three. A distinct omnibus categorical test, category-coefficient, interaction, adjusted/unadjusted variant, subgroup estimand, or timepoint may justify a distinct DIR. Multiple posterior summaries of the same identical estimand/model/timepoint are not split merely because one is a median and one an interval. Source identity unknown is not positive merge evidence.

For `dir_inference_kind`, use **one** visible classification per DIR: `bayesian_summary` when explicit posterior inference; otherwise `hypothesis_test` for an identifiable test (with or without P, estimate or interval); otherwise `inferential_estimate` for supported estimation inference; otherwise `unresolved` for eligible but unclassifiable inferential form. This is a nonoverlapping reporting classification, not a claim that testing and estimation are conceptually exclusive. Verify classification in the 9-column QC scope; derived counts are unverified if that column is hidden or the source block was not checked.

`dir_result_label` is a concise *canonical* descriptor. `pio_result_label` is a *source-local* target label and `ppo_result_label` is the wording surrounding a printed p-value. They may differ without creating new identity; relations are by stable IDs and source evidence, not by textual equality.

### 07.4 Analysis groups and lightweight Stage II context

`analysis_groups` records human-readable analysis labels, source block references, reported family/stage and evidence. Each accepted DIR has one primary `dir_analysis_group_id` when supported; missing is legitimate. An analysis group is a navigation/QC structure and does NOT certify a unique SPEC or FIT. A QC scope also is a separate human-work unit; its boundaries should normally follow one titled table, panel, meaningful source subset or source-based analysis group. Additional model-to-result associations and Q/C/SPEC/FIT reconstruction are deferred to Stage II. Preserve reported model distinctions needed for DIR identity now.

### 07.5 Relationship contracts and exceptions

`p_to_r_map` relates PPO to DIR, `pio_to_r_map` relates PIO to DIR, and `p_to_pio_map` relates PPO to PIO. Every accepted PPO has one row in each PPO-related map, resolved or unresolved; every accepted PIO has one PIO→DIR map row. Resolved references point to a known valid ID; unresolved has true missing target ID and a reason. A resolved PPO→PIO→DIR path must equal resolved direct PPO→DIR. One PIO can contain *multiple* distinct PPO expressions, each separately conserved. One DIR can have several repeated PIOs and PPOs. A single aggregate printed `all P < .05` statement covering multiple tests is **one numeric PPO expression**, and its 1:1 DIR assignment remains unresolved if no single supported DIR exists; never invent extra PIDs or a representative RID.

### 07.6 Counts and uncertainty

Derive numeric PPO, accepted PIO, DIR, DPR (distinct DIR IDs with accepted mapped numeric PPO), hypothesis-test DIR, inferential-estimate DIR, Bayesian DIR, no-P-only DIR, mapped/unresolved PPO and PIO from current verified R data and explicitly scoped source coverage. Exactly `PPO = PPO_mapped + PPO_unresolved` for p_to_r mapping, and similarly `PIO = PIO_mapped + PIO_unresolved` for pio_to_r mapping. `DIR` may be greater than PPO; `PIO` may be less than PPO. Counts must label whether model-proposed or actually human confirmed. Excluded/uncertain candidates are not included in accepted counts. Incomplete source inventory or unresolved classification prevents unqualified article-wide scientific claims; proposed row counts are NOT automatically lower bounds.


## 08. P-TO-R STATISTICAL-IDENTITY RECONCILIATION GATE

This is a distinct scientific adjudication pass **after P is frozen**, not a continuation of token extraction. It may change proposed R grouping but must not alter PIDs, raw p expressions, source observations, coordinates, or frozen source counts. Read the frozen records and original context; do not construct a new independent P extraction while assigning R.

### A. Construct source-grounded comparison context

For every P and relevant non-P result candidate gather the actual outcome/parameter, definition and time origin; contrast and reference category; population/cohort and analysis unit; test or estimator when reported; model stage; covariate adjustment/matching/stratification; source title, row, column, footnotes; and exact estimate/interval evidence when present. Use unknown for unsupported dimensions. A value's proximity to another row does not establish shared context.

### B. Search within displays and coherent analysis blocks

Keep continuation pages together. Examine possible repeated reports and distinct tests within each table/analysis block. Review Methods and footnotes rather than clustering on strings. In particular:

- Repeated P beside median and landmark survival summaries may be one global test, or distinct tests. Establish which from the source.
- Mean and median summaries of the same characteristic may share one comparison or use different tests. Do not presume either from numerical equality.
- Omnibus categorical tests and individual category-coefficient tests are distinct unless the source establishes the same reported analysis.
- Coefficients for different outcomes, reference levels, populations, time origins, model stages, or adjustment sets do not merge merely because labels/P coincide.

### C. Search across displays, main text, and supplements

For **every** frozen P, search plausible equivalents across abstract, narrative, tables, figures/captions, and supplements. Search both directions: tables to narrative/figures and narrative/figures to tables. Use source-supported analysis context to bound the candidate search. Do not claim an exhaustive all-against-all comparison of unrelated records.

Record searched scopes, plausible candidate P/R IDs examined, and disposition. If no credible counterpart is found, record `no_plausible_repeat_found` with actual inspected scope; do not fabricate a rejected pair. For large source sets, process in bounded blocks while maintaining a global candidate index so a chunk boundary cannot hide cross-block repeats.

### D. Adjudicate identity and retain uncertainty

Use `same_test` in the technical P decision ledger only when positive source evidence supports one statistical result; use `distinct_test` when a materially different result is established, or as a provisional singleton after an actual search found no plausible repeat. Use `unresolved_identity` when an assignment cannot be supported. The scientific `p_to_r_map` still uses only its permitted `resolved`/`unresolved` codes; technical dispositions are not new dictionary options.

Same numerical P is never sufficient for a merge. Different p strings do not alone prove distinct analyses. Missing context does not prove identity and does not necessarily prove difference. Document the differentiating dimension for a rejected plausible link and the positive evidence for an accepted one.

Where source identity is supported but reported values differ, keep one R and a separate `reporting_discrepancy` recording both raw literals and locators. Do not silently repair the publication or call the difference a typo. An unresolved assignment and a resolved assignment with a reporting discrepancy are different states.

**D4 — proposed multiplicity distinction.** Nominal and multiplicity-adjusted P reported for the same underlying test may share one RID when the source supports that identity. Preserve both PIDs, exact literals, correction context, and an explicit expected-reporting-variant note; their expected difference is not automatically a discrepancy. Covariate-adjusted versus unadjusted model findings remain different R identities when the analyses differ. Different formal tests/procedures addressing the same contrast are not automatically merged as two versions of one P.

Never force a minimum number of merges. A genuinely all-one-to-one mapping can be valid after documented searches. Conversely, blanket singletons or blanket unresolved assignments without those searches do not demonstrate reconciliation. Genuine residual uncertainty is permissible and must remain visible.

### E. Save the decision ledger before rendering R rows

Create exactly one technical P decision record per canonical PID: source/block references, raw expression, searched scopes, examined candidates (or explicit none), supported identity dimensions, decision, assigned RID or null, supporting/contradicting locators, concise rationale, and open issue/discrepancy links. Store actually evaluated candidate-pair decisions separately, with accepted/rejected/uncertain status and brief source-based justification. This is an auditable evidence summary, not a private deliberation transcript.

For non-P evidence, preserve source-local PIOs with qualified inferential targets. Reconcile those to existing P-associated DIRs first, then create supported non-P-only DIRs. Keep unresolved identities in the PIO→DIR ledger; an unresolved occurrence does not justify an invented DIR.

### F. Build one canonical R inventory and one P map

Create the canonical DIR inventory (P-associated and non-P-only) and PPO→DIR, PIO→DIR, and PPO→PIO links from recorded decisions; generate R review and HTML from those same current R objects. Do not re-extract or independently rebuild the scientific groupings while writing R code.

Each PID has exactly one map row. A `resolved` row has one existing RID; an `unresolved` row has missing RID and a rationale. Several PIDs may map to one RID. Non-P-only DIRs belong in the comprehensive pilot when their identity is evidenced. Stable RIDs need not be contiguous. Merges/splits preserve old-to-new ID history and do not renumber PIDs. Do not force an uncertain equivalence into a confidently distinct new RID to make counts add up.

Before finalizing, repeat the cross-display check focused on plausible unmerged repeats, narrative/abstract coverage, survival summaries, alternative summaries, and prior human decisions. This check may revise proposed R mapping only through the decision/change ledgers.

### G. Conserve P and report meaningful denominators

Compute all counts from current R-owned records. Report PPO, PIO, DIR, the DPR subset, unresolved PPO/PIO relationships, and mutually exclusive inference-kind tallies; keep test-R separate from all-DIR. Also report source-observation counts separately by format/pass, symbolic annotations separately, and candidate-search coverage. Never add representation counts to get printed P.

The invariant is `P_total = P_mapped + P_unresolved`. Unresolved P does not produce an invented DPR. Do not equate DPR with independent tests. Do not use `P_total - DPR_total` as an unconditional duplicate count. Any repeats count based on P memberships must state its denominator and exclude unresolved assignments.

A complete physical P count may coexist with unresolved R identity. If source/result inclusion or equivalence is incomplete, a final DPR total remains unresolved; a proposed mapped-DPR inventory size may still be reported as provisional. Missing results and uncertain merges mean a proposed R count is not necessarily a lower bound. Preserve both supplied-source coverage and article-wide coverage; missing cited supplements prevent an article-wide completeness claim.

