## 14. SCIENTIFIC COUNTING REGRESSION CASES — HYPOTHETICAL ONLY

A. HR + 95% confidence interval + P for one coefficient in Table 3: one PIO, one PPO, one DIR; classify test evidence and estimator together rather than three DIRs.
B. Same result in abstract and Table 3: two PIOs and two PPOs, one DIR after source-supported merge. Different numbers for same result do not automatically imply a different DIR: document discrepancy, not silent correction.
C. Unadjusted HR and covariate-adjusted HR for the same exposure: ordinarily two separate DIRs because analysis context differs, regardless of matching p-values.
D. Kaplan–Meier: one global log-rank test and each independently reported arm-specific landmark probability with confidence interval are separate DIRs, but only the global log-rank is a test DIR; grouping all together in HTML does not collapse rows.
E. Two numeric p expressions for the SAME inferential target and reporting instance (e.g. nominal and multiplicity-adjusted): one PIO, two PPO, possibly one DIR if source supports base test identity. This proves PPO can exceed PIO.
F. One aggregate printed phrase `all P < 0.05` for multiple different comparisons: preserve one numeric PPO. Qualifying PIO is accepted only if its inferential target (aggregate reporting scope) is identifiable, its PIO→DIR may remain unresolved; never fabricate component PIDs or DIRs.
G. One parameter posterior mean and credible interval: one PIO and one Bayesian DIR. Multiple posterior summaries for same estimand are one DIR absent distinct inference targets; credibility is not a frequentist confidence interval.
H. Clear inferential reporting but relationship to another result uncertain: retain accepted PIO, mapping unresolved; no invented result ID. Ambiguous descriptive/ inferential status: hold as candidate outside accepted PIO denominator.
I. The same main Table 3 appears as HTML and PDF: one logical PIO/PPO per target, with alternate source representations retained in the source ledger, not duplicate scientific counts.
J. Stage I DIR→model/Q/claim references are evidence context only. Stage II reconstructs explicit questions and claims, not invented direct one-to-one mappings.


